WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:09.200
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, welcome, everybody to our&nbsp;
board meeting. I think I should just start on a,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:15.280
well, it may be a sad note, I think, just&nbsp;
to say this is Simon’s and David Roberts’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:21.200
and Emily’s last meeting and we’ll have a proper&nbsp;
chance at the end of July when we can meet again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:28.640
in person to say a proper goodbye. But maybe&nbsp;
I could just say one thing. It is an old sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:34.160
of Black Country expression that I picked up&nbsp;
when I worked up there in the in the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:40.720
that as you all slide down the banister of life,&nbsp;
may all the splinters be pointing downhill.&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:50.160
I’m sure they will be Simon, David and Emily.
So we have apologies for absence from Dido&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:56.240
and Laura, are there any other any declarations&nbsp;
of interest over and above those already declared?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:06.640
And can we approve the minutes please? Yeah, thank&nbsp;
you very much. And so we’ll move straight on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.680
Simon’s chief executive update. Simon.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:17.040
Okay, well, thank you very much, David. And just&nbsp;
on a personal note, thank you to the boards of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:23.040
NHS England and NHS improvement, those currently&nbsp;
serving and indeed your predecessors over the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:30.160
seven and a half years. The guidance and support&nbsp;
that you provided to me and colleagues on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:37.467
executive has been hugely influential, and I think&nbsp;
has stood the NHS in good stead even though often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:42.080
is unseen to the naked eye on the part of a&nbsp;
wider group of colleagues across the service.&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:50.240
So first things first, in terms of the&nbsp;
position on coronavirus, we are seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:57.760
as a result of the increasing infection levels a&nbsp;
modest increase in the number of COVID positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:04.800
in patients across the country. But that rate&nbsp;
of increase is slowing and it is a far, far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:12.240
slower rate of increase than we saw last autumn.
So at the moment, there are just under 1,300 COVID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:21.280
positive patients in hospital compared with 34,000&nbsp;
in mid-January, and there are only around 260&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:28.320
COVID positive patients in critical care compared&nbsp;
with over 4100 in January. So I think it’s no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.680
secret why that link between infections and&nbsp;
hospitalisations, and it appears deaths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:41.360
has been broken. That of course is the vaccination&nbsp;
campaign. And there we are in this sprint to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:48.213
July 19 goal of having offered all adults the&nbsp;
opportunity for their first jab and around two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.213 --> 00:02:54.320
thirds their second jab. And as you’ll hear from&nbsp;
Emily Lawson, we are well on track for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.200
Nine and a half million&nbsp;
people in their 20s and 30s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.880
have now come forward and have their first job.
Because we’re open to all age ranges, all adults,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:12.400
we’re now able to do on demand or pop-up&nbsp;
vaccinations. We’re having another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.000
weekend of major activity across&nbsp;
the country this coming weekend.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.240
So the message is quite simple. If you haven’t&nbsp;
yet had your first job, this weekend is the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:30.160
to grab a jab. And you will see advertised widely&nbsp;
where those opportunities will present themselves.&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:36.880
And then again, I want to particularly thank&nbsp;
Emily Lawson, who as you know is changing roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:45.360
for her fantastic leadership of the NHS England&nbsp;
vaccination programme. What she will describe is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:50.880
the work now underway that we will be firing the&nbsp;
starting gun on across the health service for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:58.400
autumn booster campaign, where we are very&nbsp;
likely to be administering third COVID booster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:04.320
shots alongside flu vaccinations, subject to&nbsp;
advice from the Joint Committee on Vaccination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:09.760
and Immunisation. And that campaign, as Emily&nbsp;
will describe later on in the agenda will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.360
running from the beginning of September through to&nbsp;
mid-December, and will obviously be an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:21.840
part of the further immunological wall that we&nbsp;
want to put in the way of COVID going into winter.&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:28.320
So the pressures that are being experienced&nbsp;
across the service are not principally as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:34.480
result of COVID at the current time; they are&nbsp;
due to the rebound of normality. And a big thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:41.040
you to everybody across the health service for&nbsp;
the fact that we are back to around 91% of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:50.720
elective activity, a much faster rebound coming&nbsp;
out of the last COVID wave than we anticipated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:56.720
still an output drag linked to some of the&nbsp;
necessary infection control processes. But that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:03.040
certainly puts us in a standard is in good stead&nbsp;
for what’s going to be needed in order to ramp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.080
up activity to get waits right back down again,&nbsp;
you’ll hear more from Amanda who’s been doing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:15.280
super job leading the NHS response there, as well.
And then just two final points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:24.560
we are expecting that the legislation, the bill,&nbsp;
covering the changes that we’ve been arguing for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:29.360
in the health service will be published in the&nbsp;
next several weeks, and have a commitment from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:34.560
government that there will be a second reading&nbsp;
of that legislation in the House of Commons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:40.480
before Parliament goes down, before the&nbsp;
Commons go down on the 22nd of July.&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:48.160
And then finally, to say that in a week&nbsp;
and a bit’s time, on the fifth of July,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:51.840
of course, we will be marking the&nbsp;
anniversary of the National Health Service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:59.280
and this year, we’ll be doing so in a special&nbsp;
service of commemoration and thanksgiving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.320
which will be held at St Paul’s, and subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:08.240
to the COVID compliance that of course, St Paul’s&nbsp;
Cathedral will be operating under a number, of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.600
frontline staff and patients and volunteers from&nbsp;
across the country will be coming together to mark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:22.560
that occasion, on the fifth of July. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Simon, given this is your last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:30.720
board meeting as chief executive of the NHS, and&nbsp;
you’ve been with the NHS for most of your working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:38.800
live, if you put the way that the NHS has reacted&nbsp;
to the vaccination programme and the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:44.800
I mean, how what are your reflections&nbsp;
on the NHS as a great national public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:52.240
service? How is it formed in your view?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, the NHS throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:57.360
its existence, its 73-year existence, has&nbsp;
obviously continue to evolve and adapt. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:02.320
the reason it succeeds is because it changes.&nbsp;
There are some things that stay the same, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:07.440
essential founding principle that care is&nbsp;
available based on whether you need it rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:12.400
than whether you can pay for it. But how care is&nbsp;
delivered continues to change and that has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:18.640
obviously accelerated dramatically over the last&nbsp;
15 months under the most extreme circumstances.&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:25.920
But if you look at the things that will ultimately&nbsp;
determine the success of this health service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:32.240
it will be responsiveness to changing patient’s&nbsp;
needs and social expectations, it will be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:39.120
rapid embrace of new technology and treatments,&nbsp;
and above all, it will be backing frontline staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:45.040
which is where the magic happens. And get those&nbsp;
three things right, then there’s every reason to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:50.720
be entirely confident about the future of the NHS,&nbsp;
I believe. I mean, I joined the NHS, and I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:56.880
stop very shortly. I joined the NHS in 1988. Mrs&nbsp;
Thatcher had just kicked off a review of what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:04.800
future of healthcare should be in this country.&nbsp;
We had, there was a nurse’s pay dispute, ambulance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:12.400
strikes, and a sort of big concern about the way&nbsp;
in which care would be provided into the 1990s.&nbsp;

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:18.480
So there are these existential moments that come&nbsp;
along in the history of the NHS. You know, 1976,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:25.840
when the economic, the UK economy screwed up,&nbsp;
1988-1989, 1990 going into the 1990s and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:32.240
Ken Clarke reforms, the late 1990s when the Blair&nbsp;
administration came in and had to figure out how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:38.880
to deal with the pent-up unmet need. And I will&nbsp;
say the COVID pandemic, which has been a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:43.920
type of challenge altogether. On each of those&nbsp;
occasions, the NHS has responded fantastically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:47.840
That is only a surprise to those&nbsp;
who don’t know about the brilliance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:53.840
of the people who work in the health service.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, that’s very well said. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.840 --> 00:09:00.560
I’m just looking across at David Roberts, who has&nbsp;
been sort of steering the ship at the board level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:06.480
for seven years I think now, David, I mean, what&nbsp;
are your reflections looking back over that time?&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:13.520
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I wish you had given me notice&nbsp;
of that question, David. So I think it’s very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:26.160
similar to Simon. I am in awe at the vocation and&nbsp;
the skill and the incredible resilience and effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:33.440
of the people who work in the health service.&nbsp;
And I think it’s often the people who go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:40.080
unnoticed who are critical to making it work.&nbsp;
And I’ve told a story before of the cleaner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:46.400
who said I’m part of the infection control system&nbsp;
to allow doctors to keep people safe, and every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:54.960
time that just for me epitomises that the sense&nbsp;
of purpose and the sense of team that exists in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:02.720
all the settings where the NHS is present. So I&nbsp;
think that’s my first and overriding second thing.&nbsp;

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:09.920
The second overriding view is Simon’s&nbsp;
point about sort of adaptability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:16.000
And I think the challenge for us, as I&nbsp;
suspect will be the theme of this board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:23.440
is about how the service can increasingly&nbsp;
adapt to a very different set of consumer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:30.960
patients and citizen expectations, can capture&nbsp;
the huge opportunities from data and digital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:36.480
you know, the NHS has a very, very proud&nbsp;
history of using data to help people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:44.160
in their health, wellness, etc. And we shouldn’t&nbsp;
be scared of talking about whether it’s drug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:51.040
discovery, etc. It’s a huge opportunity to&nbsp;
help people every day, similarly, on digital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:57.440
but not at the expense of digital exclusion.
So I think adaptability is going to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:03.680
you know, at the core, as society&nbsp;
changes the way it operates hugely.&nbsp;

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:10.480
And I think the third thing is I have learned so&nbsp;
much. I’ve learned from all my colleagues at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:19.200
board, I’ve learned from Simon and the executive&nbsp;
colleagues. And I just think the experience, I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:27.840
had a hugely privileged position to be part of a&nbsp;
board that has had a small role in helping the NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:34.640
steer through some of the unparalleled challenges,&nbsp;
and I feel hugely grateful for that and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:39.520
wish everyone all the very best.
And it would be remiss of me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:47.680
to not also say a thank you to Simon, for what&nbsp;
he’s done. You know, the NHS that Simon walked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:54.800
into seven and a half years ago, and I did seven&nbsp;
years ago, is completely different from the one we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.800 --> 00:12:05.120
have now, which is much more rooted in integrated&nbsp;
care. And his ability to lead the service through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:11.680
what’s been quite a challenging period of time&nbsp;
financially and never mind from the perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:17.600
of pandemic, but also in terms of leading it from&nbsp;
one model to another model of delivery of care is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:23.840
hugely impressive. And Simon, I thank you&nbsp;
for putting up with me over the seven years.&nbsp;

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:27.840
&gt;&gt;David Prior: When you said that Simon was&nbsp;
leaving for one model to another model, I thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:32.640
you said from one muddle to another muddle,&nbsp;
but it was definitely model you said, David.&nbsp;

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:35.120
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I’ll plead&nbsp;
the fifth on that one actually.&nbsp;

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:41.840
&gt;&gt;David Prior: That’s very well said anyway, it&nbsp;
just goes to show that spontaneity is much better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:48.080
in these matters. You put it very, very nicely.&nbsp;
So shall we then move straight over into Emily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:54.400
then? Oh, Wol, you’ve got your hand up. Sorry.
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Just a very, very quick question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.400 --> 00:13:00.320
for Simon actually. So Simon, at various different&nbsp;
points, I love your sort of run through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:05.840
history of healthcare, modern healthcare in&nbsp;
the NHS over the past few decades, actually.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:11.760
As you leave and as you know, we’re all, you&nbsp;
know, grateful and thankful for what you’ve done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:15.200
what one piece of advice&nbsp;
would you give your successor?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:19.920
Not in respect of running the show, but in&nbsp;
coping with the stresses that come with it?&nbsp;

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:28.960
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Well, I mean, you&nbsp;
know, there’s a sort of highfalutin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:34.560
high minded answer there about, you know,&nbsp;
staying close to the people actually doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:38.640
clinical work at the frontline and that&nbsp;
actually gives you a lot of energy. But I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:44.080
my actual personal example would be, if you’ve&nbsp;
got young kids, they sort of keep it real.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:51.360
And, frankly, it’s a reminder that despite&nbsp;
the huge importance of everything that we do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:59.440
at work, family life and other things also matter&nbsp;
a great deal and that keeps you as grounded as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:02.080
any of the inspiration you get from&nbsp;
the fantastic colleagues you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:05.840
the privilege of working alongside.
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Very well said. Thank you.&nbsp;

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:09.520
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I thought you were going to say&nbsp;
you were leaving a message for your successor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:12.800
to say there is no more money or something&nbsp;
like that. I can’t remember who said that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:18.480
I think it was the last Labour government.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I think it’s time to cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:20.720
the chair off at this point,&nbsp;
I think we should get onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:27.600
the business in question. Yes. Thank you, David.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: You’re quite right. So should we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:33.040
move on to Emily, then please, Emily.
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:41.520
Thank you. Thank you, David. So it’s my pleasure&nbsp;
to give an update on the vaccine programme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:50.000
and to give a few comments to go over the paper&nbsp;
which outlines the successes so far, in particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:54.960
second dose acceleration and the planning for&nbsp;
winter boosters, as Simon has just outlined.&nbsp;

00:14:56.240 --> 00:15:05.600
So to just put everything back into context it’s&nbsp;
28 weeks and two days ago since Margaret Keenan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:12.800
received her jab on the eighth of December,&nbsp;
and over that period we have in England alone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:23.680
delivered 63.4 million vaccination doses, that&nbsp;
is 36.5 million who have now received just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:31.520
first dose, and 26.87 million as of this morning&nbsp;
who’ve received both the first and a second dose.&nbsp;

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:39.040
So as we’ve discussed previously, and as we&nbsp;
are very proud of being able to say in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:43.920
vaccination programme itself, you know, the&nbsp;
biggest vaccination drive in NHS history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:46.400
the fastest in Europe and the&nbsp;
most precise in the world.&nbsp;

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:54.320
You will remember from Simon’s description of the&nbsp;
programme, and our various stages of delivery back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:59.280
in the spring that we characterised our work&nbsp;
then as two sprints followed by a marathon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:09.120
We are now in a sprint at the end of that&nbsp;
marathon to reach everybody with, to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:14.480
sure we offer to everybody with the first dose,&nbsp;
and to ensure everybody has their second dose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:19.600
particularly for those aged 40 and over&nbsp;
to have their second dose at eight weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:23.680
as opposed to 11 or 12 weeks as has been&nbsp;
the case through most of the programme.&nbsp;

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:29.280
I should remind the board because I know it&nbsp;
comes up a lot when looking at the numbers that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:36.160
we are currently in a period where we&nbsp;
are eight to 11 weeks over mid-April,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:40.800
which was a point at which supplies were quite&nbsp;
limited and we did not offer a lot of first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:46.160
doses. So the reason that the second dose numbers&nbsp;
haven’t climbed recently, in fact have declined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:51.360
is because there simply aren’t the people who are&nbsp;
due a second dose at the moment. The second dose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:57.120
accuracy of delivery of people coming back in the&nbsp;
required timeframe continues to be very strong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:03.120
But we do everything we can to reach people as&nbsp;
well who don’t come back in that time period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:09.680
including reminder texts and letters as well as&nbsp;
calls from GPs to understand why somebody might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:15.760
not have returned and make sure that they do.
And so the programme continues to deliver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:22.640
every day, and in particular to confirm with&nbsp;
all communities that the offer is evergreen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.200 --> 00:17:28.240
So in the last three weeks of the programme,&nbsp;
so the three weeks that ended on Sunday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:34.960
we have in every week delivered 70,000&nbsp;
or more, in the region of 70,000 doses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:40.400
to people in cohorts one to nine who had chosen&nbsp;
now to come forward for their first dose. Last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:47.680
week that number was 73,600. So it might seem like&nbsp;
small numbers but in our mission as a programme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:52.880
which is to deliver the maximum available&nbsp;
courses of vaccination in order to minimise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:58.080
morbidity and mortality as quickly and safely as&nbsp;
possible with high uptake and priority groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:05.440
that critical mission remains on track and&nbsp;
remains important. So if anybody who has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:11.200
not yet felt able to receive a vaccination&nbsp;
now feels ready, please do come forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:18.560
This is a particularly good weekend to do that. As&nbsp;
Simon has outlined we have the grab a jab weekend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:25.280
where every system across the UK will be offering&nbsp;
multiple ways to get a vaccine including pop ups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:31.200
mobile units and additional slots that you&nbsp;
can book as well as opportunities to walk in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:38.080
whether that’s for those in those cohorts one to&nbsp;
nine to come forward for the first time, those to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.080 --> 00:18:45.200
whom vaccination has just opened up to come in, to&nbsp;
walk in and receive their first dose on time and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.200 --> 00:18:53.520
those who are in weeks eight to 12 after their&nbsp;
first dose to receive a second dose. So lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:59.840
opportunities to keep going with vaccination and&nbsp;
make sure everybody has the maximum protection.&nbsp;

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:07.200
The work that was highlighted at&nbsp;
the 5pm press conference yesterday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:15.120
to increase uptake across all groups tackling&nbsp;
as per the World Health Organization’s guidance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:20.560
the three areas critical to maximise uptake&nbsp;
of convenience, complacency and confidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:27.360
also continues and we are planning that in from&nbsp;
the beginning as we have done with this phase of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:35.440
the programme into our planning work for winter.
So if we look forward to the potential booster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:43.840
campaign for winter, we are awaiting the interim&nbsp;
guidance from JCVI, but in the meantime working on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:49.440
potential scenarios, and would expect as soon as&nbsp;
we’ve received that interim guidance to be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:55.520
write to systems with the outline planning&nbsp;
guidance, so that we can work with systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.520 --> 00:20:01.920
over the summer to plan in that winter&nbsp;
booster campaign across whatever lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:10.640
we are directed through that clinical guidance.
We are working in advance of the outcome of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:16.720
clinical studies to make sure we create the&nbsp;
opportunity to offer coadministration of flu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:21.840
and COVID vaccines, so that we can maximise&nbsp;
the uptake for both of those programmes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:28.720
and to look to further extend, and indeed,&nbsp;
turbocharge work that many systems have already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:34.880
initiated to use the opportunity of an interaction&nbsp;
around the COVID vaccine to offer other health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:41.680
interventions, including atrial fibrillation,&nbsp;
checking or learning disability health checks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:48.480
So that work needs to be done very closely&nbsp;
in collaboration with patient groups and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:53.760
local communities, because we need to make&nbsp;
sure that capacity that we’ve set up for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:58.720
autumn and winter is aligned to the needs&nbsp;
of local communities and in the capacity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:04.880
that best fits a particular health system.
So as soon as we have that planning guidance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:09.920
we have the direction from JCVI, we’ll be&nbsp;
working with systems to plan over the summer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:17.200
to make sure we have the right capacity set up. We&nbsp;
are working ahead of that guidance being available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:23.120
with St John Ambulance to make sure that the&nbsp;
volunteer task force, workforce that we’ve relied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.120 --> 00:21:29.040
on through the first part of the programme is&nbsp;
available through that winter campaign. And they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:36.080
will be looking for further volunteer vaccinators&nbsp;
and other roles to make sure that our NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:41.440
workforce capacity doesn’t have an undue strain&nbsp;
placed upon it by the addition of that booster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:46.960
campaign on top of what systems would already be&nbsp;
doing in terms of flu, and other public health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.960 --> 00:21:53.280
interventions during the autumn. So I think I will&nbsp;
stop there and I’m happy to take any questions.&nbsp;

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:59.120
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Emily.&nbsp;
Steve, would you like to comment at all?&nbsp;

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:06.880
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Yes, just a few additional&nbsp;
comments. I think Emily has outlined what we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.880 --> 00:22:12.560
doing at the moment and of course, what we plan&nbsp;
to do in the autumn contingent on JCVI advice.&nbsp;

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:20.720
So just on flu, we didn’t see any flew&nbsp;
last winter. In fact, the same experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:28.480
we’ve seen in the southern hemisphere in their&nbsp;
winter a year ago. So there is a risk that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:35.280
will get flu back with a vengeance this winter.&nbsp;
And indeed there is a risk that we will see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:43.040
other respiratory illnesses such as RSV and viral&nbsp;
infection in children that have similarly missed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:49.600
out seasons, we will see increased numbers&nbsp;
of people presenting with these infections.&nbsp;

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:56.240
So the flu campaign this week, the flu vaccination&nbsp;
campaign this autumn is going to be critical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.960 --> 00:23:01.200
As it turns out, we had a very successful&nbsp;
flu vaccine campaign last winter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:08.320
but we need to really build on that again&nbsp;
this winter to head off that threat of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.200 --> 00:23:18.000
a bad flu season. And so as Emily’s outlined,&nbsp;
being able to coadminister flu, again, contingent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:23.840
on clinical studies and relevant clinical advice,&nbsp;
I think will be a major component of driving up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:33.360
uptake rates in the over 65s in the at-risk groups&nbsp;
and any additional cohorts that we are advised to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:41.040
immunise. And so I think this autumn needs&nbsp;
obviously to be about the COVID booster programme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:46.080
but I think it’s really important that people&nbsp;
come and get their flu vaccine in the winter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:53.200
because we do want to avoid seeing a winter of&nbsp;
mixed respiratory infections at are rate higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.920 --> 00:23:58.080
than we generally see.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:01.600
thanks, Steve, I don’t know whether&nbsp;
Munir or Ara would like to come in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:07.600
at this point?
&gt;&gt;Munir Pirmohamed:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:10.800
Thanks, David. I’ll say something first. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:15.760
first thing to say is that the rollout&nbsp;
has been absolutely amazing. So you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:21.120
many congratulations to Emily and the team and&nbsp;
the people who are vaccinating at the front end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.920 --> 00:24:28.320
for delivering the vaccines and so on. I also&nbsp;
want to agree with what Simon said that, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:33.120
the vaccination campaign has been so successful&nbsp;
that we are beginning to see a severance of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:40.000
link between cases and hospitalisation and deaths.&nbsp;
And, you know, even though this delta variant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:47.627
is more transmissible, and may be causing more&nbsp;
severe disease, the vaccination is still working,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:47.627 --> 00:24:53.520
is highly effective, remarkably effective at&nbsp;
preventing severe disease and hospitalisation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:57.840
So the message still remains the same, that if&nbsp;
you’re eligible for the jab, please go and get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:01.520
the jab. If you’re waiting for a second dose,&nbsp;
then please go and get make sure you get your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.520 --> 00:25:06.080
second dose as soon as possible. So I think that’s&nbsp;
really important. We need to continue the work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:06.080 --> 00:25:10.160
and there’s a lot of work going on in the&nbsp;
background at the moment to define how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.160 --> 00:25:17.280
then move forward for the booster jobs in the&nbsp;
autumn, which individuals need to get the third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.920 --> 00:25:23.360
booster jab in the autumn to allow JCVI to&nbsp;
make the decisions based on the evidence that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:28.560
generated over the next few months.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Munir. Ara,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:32.480
did you want to comment or not?
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: Yes, David, just to express my thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:40.720
to Emily. I think you very eloquently expressed,&nbsp;
at least reassured all of us and acknowledge the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.720 --> 00:25:46.640
amazing work that Emily and the NHS have done.&nbsp;
I’ll add one other thing to it. I work in the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:47.280 --> 00:25:52.320
and I feel we’re getting back to normal. We’re&nbsp;
treating many, many other sick patients. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:56.480
anyone who wants to be vaccinated to&nbsp;
help the NHS get the patients through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:01.360
you know, if we can get that message&nbsp;
out would be tremendously helpful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:04.880
You know, I’ve been operating most of&nbsp;
the week. I can’t remember having a week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:09.840
like that for the last 14 months.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:14.560
Ara. Steve, do you want to come back?
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Yes, because I do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:19.440
think it’s worth highlighting Munir’s point&nbsp;
about second doses. Because I think it’s very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:24.640
clear that the second dose is really important&nbsp;
with the delta variant, which is becoming the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:32.880
predominant variant in the UK at the moment.&nbsp;
And so quite rightly, there is a real push&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.680 --> 00:26:38.560
for all adults now, the over-18s and of course,&nbsp;
anybody in the initial priority groups that hasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.560 --> 00:26:43.360
come forward. But I think just as important&nbsp;
is coming forward for that second dose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:54.240
So the grab a jab weekend that’s coming up and all&nbsp;
the sprints up until July 19th is aimed at those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.240 --> 00:27:00.720
who need to get their second dose as well. So if&nbsp;
you haven’t had your second dose, absolutely come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:04.800
and get it because it’s the second dose that&nbsp;
will give you that maximum protection against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:12.400
infection against severity of disease in other&nbsp;
words, hospitalisation. The first dose is good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:12.400 --> 00:27:17.520
but the second dose is the one that that is&nbsp;
really, really important in this. So don’t be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:22.880
shy if you haven’t had your second dose yet. Come&nbsp;
and get it. And Munir might want to say something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:29.360
around safety of AstraZeneca and second doses,&nbsp;
because I think it’s really important that that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:36.400
continue to make the case that you are much more&nbsp;
at risk, the second dose risk is low, very low,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:44.640
but you are much more risk of disease and severe&nbsp;
disease from infection with COVID than any safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:48.720
concerns that we’ve seen around vaccines, but&nbsp;
Munir could probably talk a little bit to that.&nbsp;

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:54.640
&gt;&gt;Munir Pirmohamed: Yes, I completely agree with&nbsp;
Steve. You know, it is really important to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:54.640 --> 00:28:00.960
the second dose. The second dose protects and&nbsp;
the data from Public Health England and various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:07.040
other organisations has shown the second dose&nbsp;
is really effective in protecting against all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.040 --> 00:28:14.000
the various including the currently dominant delta&nbsp;
variant. With regard to safety of the AstraZeneca,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:19.920
MHRS has published data and publishes data every&nbsp;
week on this, which has shown that the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.920 --> 00:28:27.040
dose of AstraZeneca is remarkably effective, and&nbsp;
safe as well. It has not been associated with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.600 --> 00:28:32.480
thrombotic thrombocytopenia events which have&nbsp;
been associated in a small number of extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:32.480 --> 00:28:36.720
rare events which have been seen with&nbsp;
the first dose. So the second dose is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.520 --> 00:28:42.800
highly effective, and very safe. So it is really&nbsp;
important that if you are due your second dose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:42.800 --> 00:28:47.520
then you should go and get that second dose.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Munir. Now, coming back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:52.880
to you Emily, you’re moving on very soon&nbsp;
now, I mean, are you happy that you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:52.880 --> 00:28:58.880
leaving behind an organisation that and the&nbsp;
people in it who can carry on the good work?&nbsp;

00:29:00.480 --> 00:29:04.640
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: I’m definitely not happy to be&nbsp;
leaving behind the organisation because it’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:05.200 --> 00:29:09.440
as I think David said earlier, I’ve learned such&nbsp;
a huge amount from the people I’ve worked with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:13.920
whether it’s directly or indirectly, over&nbsp;
the last three and a half years in the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:19.520
but and the vaccine team is pretty special,&nbsp;
both the national teams, the regional teams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.520 --> 00:29:22.960
and of course everybody else in the system&nbsp;
that way everybody has pulled together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:30.080
But I am, to the real point of your question, I’m&nbsp;
happy that we are leaving behind a really robust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:36.560
team who know what they’re doing, have really&nbsp;
good links with systems via regions and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.560 --> 00:29:39.840
brilliant leadership that’s been shown by&nbsp;
the regional directors of commissioning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:40.400 --> 00:29:44.000
in the way they’ve worked across multiple&nbsp;
boundaries, including the local authorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:48.800
to get the right capacity in the right place&nbsp;
at the right time, needs to be noted as well.&nbsp;

00:29:49.360 --> 00:29:55.600
I’m delighted to be handing over to, I won’t&nbsp;
get his titles right so I’ll just call him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:55.600 --> 00:30:03.200
Keith Willett because he has a lot of titles and&nbsp;
Keith is very able to pick up the reins and take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.200 --> 00:30:07.680
the vaccine programme forward through the winter&nbsp;
with leadership from Steve and others to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:07.680 --> 00:30:12.480
sure we’re doing everything we can to maximise&nbsp;
the opportunities for people to receive these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:15.520
lifesaving vaccines this autumn.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:15.520 --> 00:30:19.280
Well, thanks, Emily. Perhaps we could just&nbsp;
end this part of the meeting just again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:25.760
by me on behalf of everyone on the board just&nbsp;
thanking you for everything you’ve done, well, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.760 --> 00:30:32.080
three different roles, actually, since you joined&nbsp;
the NHS, but in this last role, the vaccine role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:39.040
has been by world standards, probably the best,&nbsp;
so many congratulations. Well done. And thank you.&nbsp;

00:30:39.040 --> 00:30:43.520
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Right, shall we move on then to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:43.520 --> 00:30:52.160
next item, which Amanda, you’re taking, please?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you very much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:52.160 --> 00:30:58.560
David. And if it’s okay, what I will do is give&nbsp;
an overview, because there are a couple of papers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:58.560 --> 00:31:04.240
here, which I think I’ll take together, but then&nbsp;
invite colleagues, particularly Pauline, Ian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:04.240 --> 00:31:08.880
others to come in and add detail&nbsp;
in their areas of responsibility.&nbsp;

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:14.160
But just to just to start by saying, I mean,&nbsp;
Simon has mentioned it already. But clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.160 --> 00:31:18.880
the backdrop to the operational performance&nbsp;
report is that we are in a position where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:25.040
COVID prevalence is rising. Now that is&nbsp;
having some impact on inpatient beds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:31.680
so there are now over 1000 people in hospital with&nbsp;
COVID, but of course, it’s still comparatively low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.680 --> 00:31:37.920
levels compared with what we’ve had over the&nbsp;
last 18 months. And what I think this shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.920 --> 00:31:42.240
in addition to all the comments others have made&nbsp;
is just the importance, and perhaps this is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:46.640
to be the theme, isn’t it, the adaptability and&nbsp;
the brilliance of our staff. But the importance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.640 --> 00:31:51.520
of the really excellent work actually, that&nbsp;
our primary care and community care colleagues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:51.520 --> 00:31:57.840
are doing to keep people well looked after out of&nbsp;
hospitals. So that’s partly things like the home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:04.080
oximetry service, it’s the virtual wards, it’s&nbsp;
the new drugs and treatments that they are now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:04.080 --> 00:32:10.080
increasingly being provided through primary care&nbsp;
and in the community. And so I think it’s just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:10.080 --> 00:32:15.040
a sort of huge thanks to colleagues for that.&nbsp;
But also just a recognition that this clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:15.040 --> 00:32:20.480
is something we need to keep a very close eye on&nbsp;
over the course of the next few weeks and months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:26.400
to make sure that the NHS is as resilient as&nbsp;
we need it to be over the next few months.&nbsp;

00:32:26.400 --> 00:32:32.720
And second thing, then, just in terms of kind&nbsp;
of context is, we will want to talk about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:32.720 --> 00:32:39.840
but there is just the most enormous determination&nbsp;
and energy within the NHS at the moment focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:49.840
elective recovery. And I think it’s palpable when&nbsp;
you’re out talking to people how much commitment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:49.840 --> 00:32:59.120
there is now to turning to really trying to in&nbsp;
a very, in a very determined way, deal with some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:59.120 --> 00:33:04.480
of the legacy that COVID has left us with.
But before we get onto that, I did just want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:10.560
to say, because I think it’s important, that we&nbsp;
need to just recognise that, again, contextually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:17.200
we have seen a significant increase in urgent and&nbsp;
emergency care work over the course of the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:17.200 --> 00:33:23.040
few months. So we are now back at or indeed in&nbsp;
some places above pre pandemic levels of demand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:30.960
on our urgent emergency care services. And that is&nbsp;
then compounded in practice by the impact of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:32.080 --> 00:33:37.360
infection prevention and control arrangements&nbsp;
which have an impact both on space and flow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:37.360 --> 00:33:41.840
That’s particularly acutely felt through our&nbsp;
emergency departments. But then, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:47.600
as we know, it has got an impact also on the&nbsp;
number of beds that are available to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:52.400
after patients within our hospitals as well.
And so I think what that highlights is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:52.400 --> 00:33:58.400
importance of our urgent emergency care reform,&nbsp;
a programme which Pauline is very easily leading.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:59.120 --> 00:34:03.760
That includes things like the&nbsp;
continued rollout of 111 First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:10.240
where the good news is we’re now in position where&nbsp;
30% of those people who go through 111 First and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:10.240 --> 00:34:15.520
need then to go on to emergency departments&nbsp;
are going through a booked appointment.&nbsp;

00:34:16.240 --> 00:34:21.760
We’ve also got clear evidence that the continued&nbsp;
number of patients being treated through same day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:21.760 --> 00:34:26.480
emergency care continues to rise. I think we&nbsp;
should just acknowledge the importance of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:26.480 --> 00:34:31.120
work that Steve led on the clinical review of&nbsp;
standards to which the response to consultation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.120 --> 00:34:34.560
has just recently been published. But there&nbsp;
are a couple of other things that play into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:38.720
this that are that are equally important&nbsp;
components of the UEC reform programme.&nbsp;

00:34:39.440 --> 00:34:42.080
It’s the work that’s been led around discharge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:42.640 --> 00:34:47.600
again, really importantly supported by the&nbsp;
additional funding that we’ve had available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:53.840
which has had a very significant impact on&nbsp;
reducing unnecessary length of stay in hospital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:53.840 --> 00:35:00.160
enabling people safely to go home, discharge&nbsp;
to assess services in place and supported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:00.160 --> 00:35:03.760
through that additional funding. And that’s&nbsp;
been particularly important, as I say, given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:03.760 --> 00:35:07.760
the impact of infection prevention and control&nbsp;
on the number of beds we’ve got in the system.&nbsp;

00:35:08.480 --> 00:35:11.760
But then finally, and again, I’m sure&nbsp;
Ian will want to say more about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:11.760 --> 00:35:17.200
but the commitment that our community colleagues&nbsp;
have made to getting to a position where we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:17.200 --> 00:35:22.640
got the two-hour crisis response service and&nbsp;
community services fully rolled out across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:22.640 --> 00:35:27.440
the country by the end of this financial year.
So all of those things, adding up to, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:32.640
a really important set of things to keep front and&nbsp;
centre of our minds around urgent emergency care.&nbsp;

00:35:33.520 --> 00:35:37.840
In terms of elective recovery, again, Simon’s&nbsp;
given, I think, a really helpful introduction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:44.720
we’ve seen a much more kind of rapid ramp up of&nbsp;
our elective activity over the course of the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:45.280 --> 00:35:49.120
few months than we saw certainly back&nbsp;
in the autumn period. So back as Simon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:55.040
said to sort of around about 91%.
So that is a very strong start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.040 --> 00:36:00.240
there’s no doubt that the elective recovery fund&nbsp;
has made a big difference, because it’s allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.880 --> 00:36:06.560
colleagues in the NHS to run services that have&nbsp;
cost more money in order to be able to reach that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:07.840 --> 00:36:14.560
increased level of activity more quickly. And&nbsp;
again, I think this might end up being a theme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.200 --> 00:36:18.160
colleagues might want to come in and&nbsp;
talk about it. But some of that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:18.160 --> 00:36:24.560
been necessary because of this, this impact,&nbsp;
the output impact of the infection prevention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:25.280 --> 00:36:29.680
infection, protection and control arrangements&nbsp;
that by necessity are in place at the moment.&nbsp;

00:36:30.400 --> 00:36:36.800
So for the NHS to get back to pre-pandemic&nbsp;
levels of activity and beyond, is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:36.800 --> 00:36:41.600
require and it’s clear from what colleagues&nbsp;
are already doing across the NHS already,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:41.600 --> 00:36:46.080
is going to require a number of things as set out&nbsp;
in this paper, Pauline will talk more about them.&nbsp;

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:52.400
But it’s a big focus on what we can do better&nbsp;
to manage demand. And that really is a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.400 --> 00:36:57.280
programme of initiatives supported by some of&nbsp;
the digital tools that we’ve put in place over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:57.280 --> 00:37:03.200
the pandemic, to mean that patients are able to&nbsp;
get rapid access to care, face to face absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:03.200 --> 00:37:09.920
if they need it, but digitally, if not. That&nbsp;
also means doing things like putting in place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:09.920 --> 00:37:14.640
patient initiated follow up advice and guidance&nbsp;
to reform the way that outpatient services are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.640 --> 00:37:19.840
organised. It means a significant investment in&nbsp;
diagnostics, particularly through things like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:19.840 --> 00:37:28.080
the community diagnostic hubs. It also means&nbsp;
really now an energy around putting in place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:28.080 --> 00:37:33.920
those consistent best practice pathways supported&nbsp;
by Tim Briggs and his team, particularly in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:33.920 --> 00:37:40.480
those areas around the kind of high volume, low&nbsp;
complexity work and supporting that, a real focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.480 --> 00:37:46.560
on separating green pathways and indeed green&nbsp;
sites so that they can run as efficiently as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:46.560 --> 00:37:52.720
possible to really achieve kind of upper quartile&nbsp;
indeed even upper decile levels of productivity.&nbsp;

00:37:53.440 --> 00:37:58.400
But it also means doing genuinely more, and that’s&nbsp;
about partnership with the independent sector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:58.400 --> 00:38:03.840
which continues to be a really important component&nbsp;
of our whole approach to elective recovery and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:03.840 --> 00:38:08.480
indeed, planning, of course, through autumn&nbsp;
and beyond, for where we might need, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:08.480 --> 00:38:14.480
to plan to do continuingly new work, additional&nbsp;
work on top of that very significant reform and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:19.680
transformation agenda that I’ve just outlined.
So a very strong start, but a lot more to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:23.280
And enormous thanks, I think to all of our&nbsp;
colleagues across the NHS for the energy that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:23.280 --> 00:38:28.160
they are bringing, and that is as true if you’re&nbsp;
in acute services as certainly it is if you’re in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:28.160 --> 00:38:33.040
primary care and the joint work between the&nbsp;
two, absolutely, at the crux of what we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:39.200
going to need to do to bring that sort of reform&nbsp;
energy to the whole kind of elective care journey.&nbsp;

00:38:40.240 --> 00:38:44.720
So a couple more things for me, David, if I&nbsp;
may, before I hand over. I did just want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:44.720 --> 00:38:50.240
call out cancer in particular, it’s something&nbsp;
I know the board has been very concerned about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:50.960 --> 00:38:56.240
and keen to support what we’ve done throughout&nbsp;
the pandemic, which is to give a clear steer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:57.280 --> 00:39:04.640
that cancer services needed to remain, you know,&nbsp;
an absolute priority. And I would like to say it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:04.640 --> 00:39:10.240
is clear that NHS staff have worked tirelessly&nbsp;
throughout the pandemic to maintain cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.240 --> 00:39:17.440
services. And we can see that in the data. So even&nbsp;
over the last year, we’ve seen that 2.4 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:17.440 --> 00:39:22.640
people were urgently referred for suspected&nbsp;
cancer and treatments have continued at about 98%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:22.640 --> 00:39:26.880
of the level seen the year before. And&nbsp;
that’s sort of despite that COVID pressure.&nbsp;

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:34.000
We do need though, to continue to give the&nbsp;
message that anyone who has worrying symptoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:39.520
must come forward. So even though referral levels&nbsp;
have bounced back, indeed they’re over 100%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:46.320
it’s really important I think that we just keep&nbsp;
that message that you know, the NHS is absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.320 --> 00:39:51.120
here for people, if they have any worrying&nbsp;
symptoms, and the help us help you campaign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:51.120 --> 00:39:56.240
continues to be important part of our messaging&nbsp;
around that. And then the other thing I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:56.240 --> 00:40:03.040
wanted to mention, partly because we don’t talk&nbsp;
about it that much, but it’s, it’s, it’s to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:11.040
give a particular word on screening services.
So in terms of bringing through people who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:16.560
at risk of, and indeed, then turned out to have&nbsp;
a diagnosis of cancer, breast screening services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:16.560 --> 00:40:22.240
are a particularly high-volume route. So I’m&nbsp;
really pleased that what we’ve got now again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:22.240 --> 00:40:29.040
is a huge amount of work going on from colleagues&nbsp;
across the NHS to recover breast cancer screening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:29.040 --> 00:40:35.200
alongside other screening services. But we have&nbsp;
now got plans in place to recover backlogs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:35.200 --> 00:40:39.360
get back to normal service, again by the end&nbsp;
of this financial year. So that is a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:39.360 --> 00:40:44.240
ambitious target. But great to see that&nbsp;
plans are now in place to deliver that.&nbsp;

00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:51.920
A final couple of reflections, and Ian will want&nbsp;
to say more about this. But I think we should just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:58.720
say, again, really, a really big thank you to&nbsp;
our colleagues who are working in primary care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:59.440 --> 00:41:05.520
they are working now well beyond pre COVID levels&nbsp;
of activity. And now of course, that includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:05.520 --> 00:41:09.440
a significant contribution to the vaccination&nbsp;
programme, which we’ve just been talking about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:10.080 --> 00:41:15.040
But nonetheless, they do sit as primary&nbsp;
care colleagues right at the heart of not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:15.040 --> 00:41:20.240
just the vaccination programme, not just the&nbsp;
elective recovery programme, but absolutely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:20.240 --> 00:41:25.040
about the upstream prevention work as well of&nbsp;
course as helping support people with chronic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:25.040 --> 00:41:30.400
conditions and the anticipatory care work,&nbsp;
which we know is so important to help manage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:30.400 --> 00:41:36.160
some of them that might otherwise appear as demand&nbsp;
through the urgent emergency care system. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:42.960
primary care really, really working&nbsp;
at an astonishing rate at the moment.&nbsp;

00:41:43.760 --> 00:41:48.880
And then finally, it was just to say, we’ve got&nbsp;
Claire coming later so I wasn’t going to say much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:48.880 --> 00:41:54.640
about mental health, other than to acknowledge,&nbsp;
of course, that one of the things that we expected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:54.640 --> 00:42:00.320
was to see increased demand for mental health&nbsp;
services. And indeed, that is exactly what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:00.320 --> 00:42:06.000
are seeing in the system. So particularly around&nbsp;
children and young people services, and therefore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:11.440
the additional 500 million that’s been allocated&nbsp;
to mental health service, a really important part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:11.440 --> 00:42:15.760
of making sure that we’ve got the right&nbsp;
resilience in place in mental health services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:20.400
as well, of course, as continuing to&nbsp;
deliver the long-term plan ambitions.&nbsp;

00:42:21.120 --> 00:42:26.880
Same story around learning disabilities and&nbsp;
autism and we’ve got some great success out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:27.760 --> 00:42:34.400
the focus particularly on annual physical health&nbsp;
checks. Again, Ian might want to say more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:34.400 --> 00:42:39.520
this, but it remains a really important area of&nbsp;
focus across the whole of the health service.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:40.240 --> 00:42:44.080
And indeed, we’ve got, I should&nbsp;
say, as well, Bola coming later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.080 --> 00:42:48.160
to talk to us about health inequality. So&nbsp;
rather than me give you an update on that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:48.720 --> 00:42:54.320
suffice to say that that is the theme that cuts&nbsp;
across everything. So what we’ve kept saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.320 --> 00:43:00.720
it’s a fast and fair recovery, that we are&nbsp;
all I think committed to. And that means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:00.720 --> 00:43:05.760
putting health inequalities absolutely at the&nbsp;
centre of each of the things that we’re doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:09.760
whether that’s elective recovery, whether that’s&nbsp;
mental health, whether that’s primary care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:09.760 --> 00:43:19.440
but we’ll let Bola talk about that a bit later.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much Amanda. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:19.440 --> 00:43:25.920
wonder whether Prerana and Ruth before we move on&nbsp;
maybe to Pauline could talk about activity levels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:25.920 --> 00:43:33.600
in emergency care could just comment on from their&nbsp;
perspective on the state of the workforce at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:42.080
moment within the NHS. Prerana and then Ruth.
&gt;&gt;Prerana Issar: Thank you, David. I think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:42.720 --> 00:43:51.840
three areas that I would want to highlight, one&nbsp;
is that people are seeing that recovery of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:51.840 --> 00:43:57.520
workforce, recovery of individuals and teams is an&nbsp;
absolutely essential part of recovery of services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:57.520 --> 00:44:04.640
and both of those go hand in hand in order to&nbsp;
have the destination of sustainable activity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:04.640 --> 00:44:10.560
recovery, we must have recovery of our&nbsp;
people. And in this context, people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:11.600 --> 00:44:15.680
accessing the health and wellbeing support&nbsp;
that has been put in place at local levels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:15.680 --> 00:44:20.000
at system level and at national levels.
So we have all of the mental health hubs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:20.000 --> 00:44:24.880
are operational now in every single system which&nbsp;
allow for self-referral and accelerated referral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:25.520 --> 00:44:30.160
for those who are experiencing, you know,&nbsp;
tiredness and exhaustion and stress.&nbsp;

00:44:30.800 --> 00:44:36.320
We also have a national health and wellbeing offer&nbsp;
that has been accessed more than 1 million times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:36.320 --> 00:44:40.320
which is great because we’ve been&nbsp;
working hard as a service to encourage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:40.320 --> 00:44:43.280
help and support seeking&nbsp;
behaviour because health care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:44.160 --> 00:44:50.640
workers are, the research shows, are the last to&nbsp;
seek support. So as a result of COVID I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:50.640 --> 00:44:56.400
the focus and attention on our NHS people’s&nbsp;
health and wellbeing has been brought to the fore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:56.400 --> 00:45:00.800
So that’s the first thing to say, is activity&nbsp;
recovery and people recovery going hand in hand.&nbsp;

00:45:00.800 --> 00:45:04.320
The second is we’re really focused&nbsp;
and teams are really focused on seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:04.320 --> 00:45:11.040
what are the new ways of working that are going&nbsp;
to result in effective activity recovery. And we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:11.040 --> 00:45:17.040
can see that in the 91% that Simon had&nbsp;
mentioned. So it is about more people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:17.760 --> 00:45:23.360
as we know from the people plan commitments, more&nbsp;
people working differently, and in a compassionate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:23.360 --> 00:45:28.960
and inclusive culture. So we do have more people&nbsp;
coming on board, we have the highest number of NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:28.960 --> 00:45:34.640
staff we’ve ever had in the history of the NHS at&nbsp;
this moment, both registered and non-registered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:34.640 --> 00:45:40.720
And, you know, making sure that we’re working&nbsp;
in different ways, whether that’s skill mix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:41.440 --> 00:45:46.800
or expanding roles, and bringing onboard new&nbsp;
roles. That’s the second thing is working.&nbsp;

00:45:46.800 --> 00:45:51.600
And the third is what Amanda said&nbsp;
about the inequalities agenda, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:52.240 --> 00:45:56.000
all of our people to work in a compassionate&nbsp;
and inclusive culture. And that’s a focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:56.000 --> 00:46:01.120
from a lot of the work that we’re doing with&nbsp;
leaders on, you know, standards and competencies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:01.920 --> 00:46:08.080
as well as actions from the staff survey results&nbsp;
that I know, you know, employers and trusts are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:08.080 --> 00:46:11.600
working really hard on. Thank you, David.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Prerana. And Ruth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:11.600 --> 00:46:15.600
from a nursing perspective?
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Yes, thanks very much. So I’ll just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:15.600 --> 00:46:23.120
take you back to a visit that I had in Southampton&nbsp;
last week, meeting their staff. And it was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:24.320 --> 00:46:29.360
it was a sheer joy actually to listen to them&nbsp;
when they talked about their recruitment plans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:29.360 --> 00:46:33.920
And actually, they’d moved from plans with people&nbsp;
that have, so they’ve really been concentrated on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:33.920 --> 00:46:39.600
recruiting healthcare support workers moving into&nbsp;
apprenticeships, and then into registered nurses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:39.600 --> 00:46:45.760
And that’s exactly what the rest of the NHS has&nbsp;
been doing too. And we’ve seen over 14,000 new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:45.760 --> 00:46:52.000
health care support workers start in the NHS since&nbsp;
January this year. And of course, added to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:52.640 --> 00:46:58.800
are very welcome international nurses that&nbsp;
we’ve seen, we had some ambition around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:59.360 --> 00:47:06.640
7,000 to join last year, and we’ve actually&nbsp;
got to over 11,000 new international nurses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:06.640 --> 00:47:14.720
welcoming them into our already diverse NHS.
But of course, we’ve got more to do. We have still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:14.720 --> 00:47:20.640
got to carry on recruiting and retaining&nbsp;
our NHS staff. And more at the moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:27.840
a big concentration for me is of course to&nbsp;
recruit our 1,000 new midwives that we agreed it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:27.840 --> 00:47:33.840
this board, only back in March. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: David Behan, did you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:33.840 --> 00:47:45.040
comment on this? Either no, or you’re muted David?
&gt;&gt;David Behan: No, I think Prerana has said it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:45.040 --> 00:47:49.920
all in relation to the staff and support&nbsp;
and well-being. And I think from a Health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:49.920 --> 00:47:54.160
Education England perspective, we continue to&nbsp;
support that work and think about the supply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:54.720 --> 00:48:00.800
critically how students continue to be educated&nbsp;
because they are our future supply here in the UK.&nbsp;

00:48:00.800 --> 00:48:03.440
And the priority for us is&nbsp;
just to make sure that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:04.400 --> 00:48:08.800
summer entrants into university proceed&nbsp;
and then the placements later in the year.&nbsp;

00:48:10.000 --> 00:48:14.800
Just to pick up on Ruth’s point, the student&nbsp;
nurses that will begin the third year will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:14.800 --> 00:48:19.520
have had every single year of their education&nbsp;
disrupted by COVID and their placement. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:19.520 --> 00:48:26.480
just thinking how we nurture and steward those&nbsp;
students through to fully entering the workforce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:26.480 --> 00:48:31.920
is one of the key contributions that colleagues&nbsp;
in Health Education England continue to play.&nbsp;

00:48:32.560 --> 00:48:38.160
I’d emphasise the importance of the collaborative&nbsp;
work I think HEE has been doing with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:38.160 --> 00:48:45.440
I&amp;E, largely through the people plan and the board&nbsp;
arrangements that we’ve got for that. But that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:45.440 --> 00:48:50.960
happening on a daily basis. And critically, just&nbsp;
to pick up David on the ICS point that has run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:50.960 --> 00:48:59.360
through conversations today, how we begin to align&nbsp;
with the ICS people plans to make sure that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:06.480
are generating more staff but also critically&nbsp;
picking up on the theme around transformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:06.480 --> 00:49:10.800
how those staff need to be new and different&nbsp;
and thinking about data analysts roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:10.800 --> 00:49:15.280
for instance, that are going to be critical&nbsp;
as we go forward. So we continue to work in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:15.280 --> 00:49:18.400
support. So thanks for the opportunity&nbsp;
just to highlight some of those things.&nbsp;

00:49:18.400 --> 00:49:23.520
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, David. And then,&nbsp;
Pauline, you will give the board a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:23.520 --> 00:49:29.600
bit more detail on the increase in emergency&nbsp;
and urgent care and how that may or may not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:29.600 --> 00:49:33.280
impacting on the elective programme.
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Yes, Thanks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:33.280 --> 00:49:38.800
David. And Amanda has given a really comprehensive&nbsp;
account of these papers so I don’t want to repeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:39.440 --> 00:49:44.800
what she has already said. But I think&nbsp;
just to draw the board’s attention to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:45.760 --> 00:49:52.480
the numbers, etc, around our attendances&nbsp;
and our admissions from a UEC perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:52.480 --> 00:49:59.680
and clearly those numbers have continued to grow.&nbsp;
And I think as we’re all aware, we are now back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:59.680 --> 00:50:06.000
attendances and admissions that were as great if&nbsp;
not greater than the numbers we saw before COVID.&nbsp;

00:50:06.800 --> 00:50:10.000
So clearly, our focus is&nbsp;
on supporting the service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:10.640 --> 00:50:17.600
and focusing on what we can do through demand&nbsp;
management, supporting patients through 111,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:19.120 --> 00:50:26.640
a significant increase in 111 calls each&nbsp;
month. And really looking at how we can open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:26.640 --> 00:50:33.760
new pathways for patients who do contact&nbsp;
111, be it online or by telephone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:34.880 --> 00:50:40.160
Opening some of the pathways that did&nbsp;
close for IPC reasons and other reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:40.160 --> 00:50:46.320
And I think really importantly, increasing&nbsp;
the level of clinical capacity to support 111.&nbsp;

00:50:47.200 --> 00:50:52.320
At the same time, clearly, we’ve had a&nbsp;
focus on supporting the ambulance service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:53.040 --> 00:50:58.480
using technology, but also increasing&nbsp;
the amount of calls that they are able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:58.480 --> 00:51:03.360
to do by increasing workforce, and the&nbsp;
number of vehicles that are on the road.&nbsp;

00:51:04.400 --> 00:51:09.440
And within hospital, I think it’s the work&nbsp;
that Amanda referred to around same day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:09.440 --> 00:51:15.920
emergency care, and our clinical pathways,&nbsp;
and working very closely with Ruth on IPC.&nbsp;

00:51:15.920 --> 00:51:22.720
So I think there’s a whole range of things taking&nbsp;
place, to support the increased attendances and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:22.720 --> 00:51:29.040
admissions. And all of that comes together&nbsp;
in the emergency care strategy. And I’m very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:29.040 --> 00:51:34.400
grateful that the board has focused on this,&nbsp;
as well as on the elective care strategy.&nbsp;

00:51:34.400 --> 00:51:39.840
I think as far as the elective care strategy is&nbsp;
concerned, maybe the only thing that I would add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:39.840 --> 00:51:46.480
to what Amanda said is just to acknowledge&nbsp;
the excellent work that’s being done by our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:46.480 --> 00:51:54.000
accelerator sites. We’ve set out that we’ve got&nbsp;
12 accelerator sites, working across that 12-week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:54.000 --> 00:52:01.280
programme, and also five children’s hospitals as&nbsp;
the 13th. And what they’re actually doing as set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:01.280 --> 00:52:08.480
out in the paper is looking at new models of care,&nbsp;
looking at innovation, how we can better improve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:08.480 --> 00:52:15.440
our processes, how we can increase capacity,&nbsp;
in order that we can do more elective work.&nbsp;

00:52:15.440 --> 00:52:20.880
And I think, you know, working with them on a&nbsp;
weekly basis, we can see how they are taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:21.920 --> 00:52:27.520
learning and applying it locally. So it’s very&nbsp;
much an adapt and adopt methodology that they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:28.320 --> 00:52:35.040
using. And it covers everything from working&nbsp;
closely with primary care, it’s really integrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:35.040 --> 00:52:41.520
with primary care, to things like running&nbsp;
better waiting list management, etc, validation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:41.520 --> 00:52:48.400
of waiting lists, looking at the work that&nbsp;
clinicians are doing about ongoing prioritisation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:48.400 --> 00:52:55.520
appropriate use of IS, implementing the work that&nbsp;
Amanda referred to earlier around high volume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:55.520 --> 00:53:00.080
low complexity work, etc, etc. I could go&nbsp;
on, but they’re basically they’re taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:00.080 --> 00:53:04.720
all of those things and applying them within&nbsp;
those 12 systems and then sharing the learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:04.720 --> 00:53:10.800
with us. I think that’s all I need to add.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much Pauline. And Ian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:10.800 --> 00:53:18.000
maybe you could give the board a view on the&nbsp;
extent to which all the initiatives we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:22.240
taking outside the hospital, I’m thinking about&nbsp;
discharge to assess, hospital at home, those kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:22.240 --> 00:53:27.840
of projects, the kind of aggregate impact you&nbsp;
think that’s having on the sort of hospital take.&nbsp;

00:53:27.840 --> 00:53:37.520
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you, David. Look, as the shape&nbsp;
of the pandemic changes with the delta variant and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:37.520 --> 00:53:42.320
given our vaccination success, so the shape&nbsp;
of the NHS response is changing as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:42.320 --> 00:53:47.760
with a far bigger increased focus on what&nbsp;
primary and community services can do.&nbsp;

00:53:47.760 --> 00:53:53.440
If you look at, for example, the response in&nbsp;
Bolton, you know, we’ve had the full rollout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:53.440 --> 00:54:00.800
of oximetry at home and COVID virtual wards that&nbsp;
will stand us in good stead a greater proportion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:00.800 --> 00:54:06.400
of the burden of responding to COVID, I think,&nbsp;
will fall to primary and community services.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:07.200 --> 00:54:14.080
And you flagged particularly the discharge work.&nbsp;
So I had updated the board previously that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:14.080 --> 00:54:19.040
discharge to assess work, including the funding&nbsp;
we’ve had during the pandemic and for H1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:19.040 --> 00:54:26.720
has freed up in a sustainable way the&nbsp;
equivalent of over 6000 staffed NHS beds. That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:26.720 --> 00:54:33.200
the equivalent of more than 12 district general&nbsp;
hospitals. That capacity has been absolutely vital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:33.760 --> 00:54:39.840
to enable the NHS to recover activity levels&nbsp;
to the extent it has and will be of critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:39.840 --> 00:54:46.880
importance as we continue through the elective&nbsp;
programme. The NHS I think is across all regions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:46.880 --> 00:54:54.240
and all systems incredibly focused on maximising&nbsp;
the use of that funding, maintaining and where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:54.240 --> 00:55:00.640
it can improving the length of stay dividend.
It’s also been great in community services to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:01.280 --> 00:55:08.800
the rollout of the urgent two-hour response&nbsp;
services and the increasing join up with same day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:08.800 --> 00:55:16.640
emergency care. As Amanda said, in general&nbsp;
practice, it has never been busier. Our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:16.640 --> 00:55:22.400
colleagues in general practice have been working&nbsp;
incredibly hard, both on the vaccination programme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:22.400 --> 00:55:28.560
continuing to offer urgent care and access to a&nbsp;
variety of different modalities, and now focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:28.560 --> 00:55:34.080
on the backlog of chronic disease management,&nbsp;
as well as other preventative services. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:34.080 --> 00:55:40.560
I’d particularly highlight the success of&nbsp;
PCNs also in securing additional capacity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:40.560 --> 00:55:47.360
So they are ahead of schedule in terms of hiring&nbsp;
staff under the additional roles scheme. Our best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:47.360 --> 00:55:56.240
data shows that over 9100 FTEs were in post by&nbsp;
the end of March last year. And we will need all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:56.240 --> 00:56:02.800
the capacity we can get in general practice.
And then finally, I just wanted to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:03.840 --> 00:56:08.080
despite the busyness of general practice,&nbsp;
I think there’s been a huge recognition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:08.960 --> 00:56:14.400
of the critical importance of providing better&nbsp;
health care to people with learning disability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:14.960 --> 00:56:20.320
This was true and flagged in the long-term plan&nbsp;
where we had a goal from increasing the proportion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:20.320 --> 00:56:27.440
of physical health checks for people with learning&nbsp;
disability problems up from 50% to 75%. So despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:27.440 --> 00:56:32.880
all of these other pressures, we asked general&nbsp;
practice to see if it could accelerate the rollout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:32.880 --> 00:56:39.280
of this during the course of the last year, and&nbsp;
set as 67% goal in our plan on inequalities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:40.080 --> 00:56:46.240
And that goal has been comfortably exceeded.&nbsp;
At the end of March last year, we were at 73.5%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:47.520 --> 00:56:54.080
against the long-term planning goal of achieving&nbsp;
75% by 2024. So we’re within a hair’s breadth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:54.080 --> 00:56:59.440
of achieving that really important goal two&nbsp;
years early. And I think that’s a testimony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:59.440 --> 00:57:03.280
to the fact that general practices&nbsp;
really wanted to focus on some of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:04.400 --> 00:57:11.840
priority areas that do not always receive the same&nbsp;
headline attention as others. Thank you, David.&nbsp;

00:57:11.840 --> 00:57:17.920
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Ian. I was just looking&nbsp;
at Tim Ferriss, given your experience in the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:17.920 --> 00:57:23.440
and how, what they’ve learned from the pandemic,&nbsp;
in terms of treating more people outside hospital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:23.440 --> 00:57:28.320
settings and the like. Are there any sort of&nbsp;
useful lessons that we could learn from them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:28.320 --> 00:57:33.520
or they could learn from us, do you think?
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: Well, I think, David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:33.520 --> 00:57:40.240
thanks for the question. And I think, honestly,&nbsp;
they’re learning more from us, in many ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:40.240 --> 00:57:50.080
because there is increasing centralisation of&nbsp;
and the use of data and analytics in the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:51.600 --> 00:57:58.880
to drive, to use assets optimally. I think,&nbsp;
what Emily showed me the other day about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:59.440 --> 00:58:05.760
ventilator use during the pandemic, that&nbsp;
kind of active asset management, where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:06.320 --> 00:58:12.160
actually know exactly what exists across the&nbsp;
patch, if I can use your expression. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:13.680 --> 00:58:19.920
and then level loading, which&nbsp;
I, which is another term that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:19.920 --> 00:58:24.880
is a term applied to some of the things&nbsp;
that Amanda and Pauline just described.&nbsp;

00:58:26.320 --> 00:58:35.360
But that happens at scale, when you have scale,&nbsp;
right? And the NHS has scale. And so often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:35.360 --> 00:58:43.600
in the US, they’re actually trying to achieve&nbsp;
scale, because the system is built on a largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:43.600 --> 00:58:51.520
many little providers that are not joined up.&nbsp;
So I think the NHS has a big advantage there.&nbsp;

00:58:51.520 --> 00:58:57.280
On the other hand, they are further along&nbsp;
in the adoption of digital technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:57.840 --> 00:59:07.680
And that has, I think that’s where we have some&nbsp;
real opportunities. But based on my initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:08.960 --> 00:59:19.200
month here with the NHS, there are, I am&nbsp;
able to as a patient in the US manage my care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:19.200 --> 00:59:26.720
over my phone, and really all aspects of my&nbsp;
care. That is, it’s now clearly shown and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:28.160 --> 00:59:34.400
Matthew Gould and NHS X and the D teams have&nbsp;
clearly shown that that is possible, but it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:35.920 --> 00:59:42.400
as widely disseminated throughout the NHS&nbsp;
with as many functions as it could be. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:42.960 --> 00:59:51.120
opportunities are there. The pandemic has shone&nbsp;
a light on those opportunities. And I think to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:51.120 --> 00:59:59.520
just refer back to what Stephen said earlier, the&nbsp;
challenges are perennial, but the opportunities in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:59.520 --> 01:00:05.200
front of us are tremendous. And it’s a terrific&nbsp;
team that’s driving towards those opportunities.&nbsp;

01:00:05.200 --> 01:00:09.520
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And Tim is good to hear&nbsp;
that when you use the word us, you mean us.&nbsp;

01:00:12.080 --> 01:00:14.400
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: I’m already there, David.&nbsp;

01:00:16.160 --> 01:00:19.520
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Wol?
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: I just have a quick question&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:19.520 --> 01:00:28.080
for Ian, just thinking back to the children with&nbsp;
special education needs and the work we’re doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:28.080 --> 01:00:33.920
to support them. I was just interested because&nbsp;
of course, this is involving schools as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:33.920 --> 01:00:40.480
isn’t it? And schools are an important part of&nbsp;
getting the right picture. To what extent are we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:40.480 --> 01:00:45.040
sort of engaging and making sure we understand&nbsp;
local authority schools, and that whole piece&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:45.040 --> 01:00:50.240
around how people are being looked after?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:50.240 --> 01:00:56.880
Thank you, Wol. So my update was particularly&nbsp;
on people with learning disability in general&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:56.880 --> 01:01:01.920
practice. We know that there is much&nbsp;
more that can be done to support people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:01.920 --> 01:01:08.880
with LD and also people with autism. And the NHS&nbsp;
has a huge role to play in terms of diagnosis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:09.600 --> 01:01:16.320
but also as a partner exactly as you say, with&nbsp;
schools and with the education department that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:16.320 --> 01:01:21.840
leads on providing better services for&nbsp;
children with special educational needs.&nbsp;

01:01:22.400 --> 01:01:29.920
And just within NHSEI. I’ve recently supported&nbsp;
Matthew Wynn to try and strengthen his team&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:29.920 --> 01:01:36.800
so that he can have a far greater focus on this&nbsp;
area, working alongside Claire Murdoch. Because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:36.800 --> 01:01:42.880
think very few people would disagree that children&nbsp;
with special educational needs do not receive&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:44.000 --> 01:01:51.840
the services that they ideally should&nbsp;
across the public sector taken as a whole.&nbsp;

01:01:51.840 --> 01:01:59.520
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks Ian. Well, Amanda, thank you&nbsp;
very much for presiding over this session. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:00.400 --> 01:02:04.720
we’ve really got to approach the elective&nbsp;
programme in the same way that we approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:04.720 --> 01:02:08.880
the vaccine programme, haven’t we, over the last&nbsp;
year. It’s got to be an absolute priority for us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:09.440 --> 01:02:14.880
Of course, that’s not to say that emergency and&nbsp;
urgent care aren’t just as important. But that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:15.680 --> 01:02:23.040
backlog of work, both diagnostic and treatment&nbsp;
work is just huge, and very, very important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:23.920 --> 01:02:28.400
So thank you all very much for that.
Shall we move on then to the system&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:28.400 --> 01:02:33.360
oversight framework?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:33.360 --> 01:02:37.120
David, we can do. Did you want&nbsp;
to cover the financial report?&nbsp;

01:02:37.120 --> 01:02:40.560
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Oh, I’m sorry,&nbsp;
Julian, I’ve completely omitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:42.400 --> 01:02:50.320
you there. Sorry about that. Julian.
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: Happy to be omitted. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:50.320 --> 01:02:57.200
the report, I’ll assume the paper is read. The&nbsp;
key points that I would highlight, I mean, look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:57.200 --> 01:03:02.560
clearly, the government have made available&nbsp;
substantially more funding as we went through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:03.600 --> 01:03:10.880
last year and the pandemic and indeed, in&nbsp;
recognition of the sort of pandemic, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:10.880 --> 01:03:15.840
infection prevention control sort of output&nbsp;
gap that I think we have already referred to.&nbsp;

01:03:18.400 --> 01:03:22.480
Against the money that we gave to systems,&nbsp;
which is clearly where the bulk of the money&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:22.480 --> 01:03:33.600
goes in administration to commissioners and&nbsp;
providers, they basically delivered a surplus&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:33.600 --> 01:03:40.480
of about £250 million against the money we&nbsp;
provided, which is close to £138 billion. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:42.880 --> 01:03:51.120
it’s a really effective use of resource.
Now in the tables, in the paper I’ve provided,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:51.920 --> 01:03:56.960
it compares it to the plans that people&nbsp;
originally set. So you actually see sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:56.960 --> 01:04:01.600
of a bigger surplus delivered. But that is&nbsp;
because when we got the initial plans back&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:02.320 --> 01:04:08.240
for use of the funds provided in September, for&nbsp;
the second half of the year, people were flagging&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:08.240 --> 01:04:13.120
to us that they were concerned that they would run&nbsp;
a deficit. That isn’t what happened. They actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:13.120 --> 01:04:17.840
came in just under the allocation. So that’s the&nbsp;
difference between if you look at the provider&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:17.840 --> 01:04:24.000
side, for example, a number close to £1 billion&nbsp;
difference between planning and outturn. That is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:24.960 --> 01:04:29.200
because they were showing to us a risk that they&nbsp;
would overspend relative to the allocations given.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:37.520
In total, it was £250 million system surplus.
Now in addition, the government provided&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:37.520 --> 01:04:43.280
significant additional funds for some national&nbsp;
interventions that we put in place, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:43.280 --> 01:04:48.480
things like the contracts you put in place in&nbsp;
dependency to hospitals, the enhanced discharge&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:48.480 --> 01:04:54.800
regime we have just spoken about, the Nightingale&nbsp;
hospitals and a series of other things.&nbsp;

01:04:56.480 --> 01:05:01.040
When we originally agreed with government&nbsp;
to put those interventions in place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:01.040 --> 01:05:05.360
clearly often quite early on in the&nbsp;
pandemic, or indeed, in the autumn, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:06.400 --> 01:05:10.480
there was a lot of uncertainty about what&nbsp;
would actually happen through the balance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:10.480 --> 01:05:17.680
the year. So clearly, we were making estimates for&nbsp;
potential costs. The government provided funding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:19.280 --> 01:05:28.240
against those sort of initial estimates, and our&nbsp;
clear commitment was to live within those and only&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:28.240 --> 01:05:33.600
use them to the degree that was necessary.
So against each of those sort of ringfenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:35.120 --> 01:05:45.360
allocations, we have come in under the&nbsp;
total amount sort of initially estimated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:45.360 --> 01:05:52.560
which I think is a reflection of good tight&nbsp;
operational and financial management control.&nbsp;

01:05:53.600 --> 01:05:58.800
In addition, we held a series of&nbsp;
sort of provisions against risk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:58.800 --> 01:06:08.720
because we said to systems to plan and&nbsp;
live within the envelopes we gave them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:09.840 --> 01:06:18.000
but there was significant uncertainty around&nbsp;
things like annual leave. We had a series of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:18.000 --> 01:06:25.040
legal cases we were in the process of closing&nbsp;
out. You know, so there was a series of kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:25.040 --> 01:06:33.280
of risk provisions that we were holding&nbsp;
centrally, that we said we would cover. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:34.240 --> 01:06:39.360
on all of those, we have not needed to&nbsp;
deploy all of the risk provision that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:40.720 --> 01:06:51.200
we had been holding, which is the right answer.
So in total, that is why there is a difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:51.200 --> 01:07:01.040
between the amount of money in the estimate and&nbsp;
the total outturn, but broadly, systems spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:01.040 --> 01:07:06.400
the money that we gave them, good control of the&nbsp;
national programmes, and we have not needed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:06.400 --> 01:07:13.280
deploy all the risk provision that we have held.
Whereas on capital, clearly, again, the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:13.280 --> 01:07:19.200
provided quite a lot of extra investment, not&nbsp;
only to deal with the consequences of COVID,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:19.200 --> 01:07:24.320
where we put in more beds, critical&nbsp;
care, infrastructure, diagnostic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:26.320 --> 01:07:31.760
equipment, and so on. They also provided&nbsp;
additional funding for us to catch up on backlog&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:33.120 --> 01:07:41.280
maintenance and to expand ED capacity and to&nbsp;
begin to get rid of mental health dorms. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:41.280 --> 01:07:47.760
on all of those were pretty much on track with the&nbsp;
investment providers. So I think as you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:48.640 --> 01:07:55.840
we have pretty much used the funding that&nbsp;
we were given. If there are any questions?&nbsp;

01:07:57.200 --> 01:08:03.360
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Julian. Kind of just one&nbsp;
question just on Mike Richards’ diagnostic hubs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:04.160 --> 01:08:14.560
how much progress are you’re making with them?
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: So, we have a plan. We have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:14.560 --> 01:08:22.400
business case, which we are just about to put&nbsp;
to the Department of Health and HM Treasury.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:23.520 --> 01:08:28.160
If that gets approved, I think it’s an investment&nbsp;
committee with the department in about two weeks’&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:28.160 --> 01:08:39.920
time, that would enable us to put a community&nbsp;
diagnostic hub in every system in the country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:39.920 --> 01:08:47.520
That would increase our diagnostic capacity around&nbsp;
things like CT and MRI, by about 8%, I think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:47.520 --> 01:08:55.280
number is compared to today. So it’s, we’ve got&nbsp;
a plan to get started. We have the case ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:55.280 --> 01:09:00.320
for kind of cross government approval, David.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay, thanks. Thanks, Julian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:00.960 --> 01:09:07.440
Any other questions for Julian? Okay, I&nbsp;
think Amanda we’ve covered the third paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:07.440 --> 01:09:12.240
hadn’t we, the COVID response really. Yeah.&nbsp;
Excellent. So should we move on to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:12.240 --> 01:09:16.880
system oversight framework? Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Great. Thank you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:16.880 --> 01:09:25.280
And so just to kind of set again the context for&nbsp;
this, so colleagues will recall that we went out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:25.280 --> 01:09:31.040
to formal consultation on the new single oversight&nbsp;
framework, which really proposed three sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:31.040 --> 01:09:38.400
significant changes from the previous version. So&nbsp;
what it suggested was a move away from something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:38.400 --> 01:09:43.440
that focused on only single organisations to&nbsp;
something that allowed us to have a system view.&nbsp;

01:09:44.080 --> 01:09:48.800
Secondly, it brought together finance and&nbsp;
quality special measures to create a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:48.800 --> 01:09:55.360
regime recovery support, which brought both&nbsp;
of those things together. And then thirdly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:55.360 --> 01:10:00.720
it focused on a balanced scorecard approach&nbsp;
against six domains that align with a long-term&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:00.720 --> 01:10:06.720
plan, but also align with what we have said are&nbsp;
the kind of key purpose and focus of integrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:06.720 --> 01:10:13.680
care systems, and include the ability to reflect&nbsp;
in that things like health inequalities. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:13.680 --> 01:10:19.040
this is a really important building block of&nbsp;
our kind of architecture as we move towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:19.760 --> 01:10:25.840
much more a kind of delivery through systems,&nbsp;
but also brings us right up to date with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:25.840 --> 01:10:29.680
priorities that we have committed to in the&nbsp;
long-term plan and, of course, all the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:29.680 --> 01:10:33.920
we’ve already been talking about today.
So that’s the context. The reason I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:33.920 --> 01:10:37.200
bringing the paper back today is&nbsp;
that we’ve now of course completed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:37.200 --> 01:10:41.360
the consultation. So we have the results,&nbsp;
and we’re bringing this here for approval.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:41.920 --> 01:10:47.840
So a really good response to the consultation,&nbsp;
we had over 120 responses from individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:47.840 --> 01:10:54.720
organisations and groups, we had also a series&nbsp;
of conversations and roundtables to support that.&nbsp;

01:10:55.280 --> 01:10:59.920
What came through was a strong support for&nbsp;
the direction of travel. So in particular, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:59.920 --> 01:11:07.200
system-based approach, really liked the idea of&nbsp;
a phased and flexible approach to implementation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:07.760 --> 01:11:13.840
We also heard positive feedback about the balanced&nbsp;
scorecard approach and the six oversight themes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:13.840 --> 01:11:19.440
aligned to the long-term plan. And again, positive&nbsp;
feedback on the move away from special measures to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:19.440 --> 01:11:26.640
this new recovery support programme structure.
However, we did get some very important feedback&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:26.640 --> 01:11:32.320
in three areas. So a request for further&nbsp;
clarification on accountability arrangements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:32.320 --> 01:11:36.400
particularly for this year, given it’s&nbsp;
a transition year as we anticipate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:37.040 --> 01:11:43.760
legislation. Secondly, we had a clear call&nbsp;
for clarification about how primary care&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:43.760 --> 01:11:48.240
performance and quality would be considered.
And then I think the third point was about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:48.240 --> 01:11:52.160
wanting to be more explicit about&nbsp;
the how this fits in and aligns with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:52.160 --> 01:11:58.400
wider oversight regulatory arrangements.
And so taking all of that into account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:58.400 --> 01:12:05.680
we’ve updated the document, the single oversight&nbsp;
framework now in a number of different ways. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:05.680 --> 01:12:12.240
the introduction has been updated to clarify the&nbsp;
existing statutory accountability arrangements&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:12.240 --> 01:12:17.280
continue to apply. We’ve also made a much&nbsp;
clearer link between this and the future&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:17.280 --> 01:12:23.440
direction of travel with legislation and the ICS&nbsp;
design framework that was just published very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:23.440 --> 01:12:29.440
recently. We’ve increased the emphasis on primary&nbsp;
care and PCNs and included clarification that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:29.440 --> 01:12:36.240
primary care performance and quality will be a key&nbsp;
part of oversight at both place and system level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:36.240 --> 01:12:41.920
We’ve updated the dedicated section on alignment&nbsp;
with partners now to include reference to health&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:41.920 --> 01:12:46.800
and wellbeing boards, and local authority,&nbsp;
health overview and scrutiny committees.&nbsp;

01:12:47.920 --> 01:12:54.080
So hopefully, that’s just a quick run through&nbsp;
the changes so that people don’t have to trawl&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:54.080 --> 01:12:59.520
through and work out what’s different between the&nbsp;
two documents. But it’s once again, been a really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:59.520 --> 01:13:04.080
I think, a really helpful lesson in&nbsp;
how useful consultation can be because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:04.640 --> 01:13:10.320
I think we have been able to reflect some&nbsp;
really helpful and important points in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:10.320 --> 01:13:15.200
proposed single oversight framework as a result&nbsp;
of that consultation and engagement process.&nbsp;

01:13:15.200 --> 01:13:21.600
And so next steps, David, assuming that the&nbsp;
board, and this is what we’re asking you to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:21.600 --> 01:13:27.120
are content to approve this, then the&nbsp;
next step will be obviously to issue that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:27.120 --> 01:13:33.200
to begin implementation. But importantly, we&nbsp;
also are absolutely committed to continue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:33.200 --> 01:13:39.040
working with leaders from across the NHS, as well&nbsp;
as partner organisations to further develop the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:39.040 --> 01:13:43.760
approach to oversight for future years.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Amanda.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:44.480 --> 01:13:52.000
Andrew, you were instrumental in setting up the&nbsp;
ICS at Frimley. Are you comfortable with this sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:52.000 --> 01:13:58.000
of set of directions encompassed in this report?
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: Absolutely. I think it sets out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:58.000 --> 01:14:02.880
all of the key things a lot of leaders want to&nbsp;
see. And it’s a great example of co-production.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:04.240 --> 01:14:12.240
So I know Amanda’s had, you know, some great&nbsp;
input from ICS leaders. And the document&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:12.240 --> 01:14:20.240
gives absolute clarity around key things like&nbsp;
accountability, the money flow, and how we expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:20.240 --> 01:14:26.240
people to behave and respond to our delivery&nbsp;
agenda going forward. So I think it’s a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:26.240 --> 01:14:32.560
good document and credit to everyone involved.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Andrew. Simon, I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:32.560 --> 01:14:38.160
wondered, when you wrote all those years ago&nbsp;
the five year forward view and you were sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:38.160 --> 01:14:43.440
positing greater integration, is this, do you feel&nbsp;
that we’ve pretty much arrived at the destination&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:43.440 --> 01:14:49.120
you had in mind all those years ago?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:49.120 --> 01:14:53.200
Arrived? No. But a long way down the&nbsp;
track? Yes. Because if you think back to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:53.920 --> 01:15:01.360
you know, the not just the 2012&nbsp;
inception, but actually back to 1991,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:01.920 --> 01:15:08.320
we’ve hardwired in the arm wrestle between&nbsp;
different parts of the service. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:09.120 --> 01:15:14.480
that was not particularly helped by having&nbsp;
a separate foundation trust regulator that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:14.480 --> 01:15:21.120
was separate from a NHS Trust regulator, which was&nbsp;
separate from what NHS England was doing. And so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:21.120 --> 01:15:27.280
think it’s quite easy because our eyes are always&nbsp;
focused forward on the horizon to remember out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:27.280 --> 01:15:32.480
of the rear-view mirror, the journey we’ve come,&nbsp;
and the amount of frictional cost, wasted energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:33.040 --> 01:15:39.920
and wave interference that went on from having&nbsp;
those two or three different ways of trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:41.600 --> 01:15:45.440
create shared purpose and improvement across&nbsp;
the service. I mean, that is now well and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:45.440 --> 01:15:49.840
truly behind us. And thank goodness for that.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks. And, Tim, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:49.840 --> 01:15:54.960
given your experience of accountable care&nbsp;
organisations in the US, do you? Do you feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:54.960 --> 01:16:01.280
that this we’ve got the right structure here?
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: David, I’m so enthusiastic about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:01.280 --> 01:16:08.960
this structure, it’s probably not an exaggeration&nbsp;
to say it is maybe besides the team of people that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:08.960 --> 01:16:16.640
I’m working with the most important reason why I&nbsp;
chose to come here. It is, it’s the fragmentation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:16.640 --> 01:16:24.640
of care and the toll that that fragmentation of&nbsp;
care takes on all of the people who receive care&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:25.200 --> 01:16:33.840
is extraordinary. And even with the best&nbsp;
technology, this expression actually from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:34.560 --> 01:16:43.120
England of joined up care is an aspiration that&nbsp;
is very, very difficult to achieve, when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:45.520 --> 01:16:50.720
different parts of the delivery system which&nbsp;
from an individual person’s perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:51.280 --> 01:16:56.800
are part of their journey, but from the&nbsp;
organisational perspective are different silos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:57.360 --> 01:17:06.000
is clearly not in the best interests of&nbsp;
the citizens of England or of any place.&nbsp;

01:17:06.720 --> 01:17:15.280
And so it is, I think, really exciting to be&nbsp;
sitting on the cusp of what is an extraordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:15.280 --> 01:17:23.600
organisational change that puts the patient&nbsp;
at the centre of care. And then will allow us&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:24.160 --> 01:17:33.200
to build the systems around that care to make&nbsp;
it to use Simon Stevens’, frictionless. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:33.200 --> 01:17:38.320
I guess I’m turning it around. He said, there’s&nbsp;
too much friction. So the goal is frictionless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:38.320 --> 01:17:47.760
And, and that is, it’s the structures that are&nbsp;
now being put in place through this process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:48.800 --> 01:17:58.240
they are necessary. They’re not sufficient, I’ll&nbsp;
just point out, but they are necessary to allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:58.240 --> 01:18:05.200
that to happen. So I, David, I’m really quite&nbsp;
enthusiastic as you can tell about these changes.&nbsp;

01:18:05.200 --> 01:18:09.440
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Thank you very much,&nbsp;
Tim, that’s really helpful. So Amanda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:09.440 --> 01:18:13.760
thank you very much for that. I think,&nbsp;
is the board happy to approve the paper?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:14.720 --> 01:18:19.440
Yeah? Good. Excellent. So I think we’ll leave with&nbsp;
those words of Tim, necessary but not sufficient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:19.440 --> 01:18:28.400
ringing in our ears. Good. Thank you very much.&nbsp;
So is Tim Kendall, is Claire and Tim here?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:30.800 --> 01:18:43.520
I can’t see Tim. Are they here? Claire, are&nbsp;
you there? Hello? Or Tim, are you there?&nbsp;

01:18:43.520 --> 01:18:45.360
&gt;&gt;Tim Kendall: I’m here.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Hi, Tim.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:46.880 --> 01:18:51.440
Tim, why don’t you introduce yourself to everybody&nbsp;
because you won’t know everybody on the call&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:51.440 --> 01:18:54.880
while we try and find Claire?
&gt;&gt;Tim Kendall: Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:54.880 --> 01:19:02.160
I’m Tim Kendall. I’m national clinical director&nbsp;
for mental health. But I’m also a psychiatrist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:02.160 --> 01:19:08.160
working with homeless people in Sheffield.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Tim. is Claire there now?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:12.800 --> 01:19:15.200
I think she’s on the call, but we can’t hear her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:20.560 --> 01:19:27.360
Amanda, is it fair to ask you to introduce this&nbsp;
and then for Tim to carry on? Or do you want us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:27.360 --> 01:19:30.960
try to locate Claire?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:30.960 --> 01:19:36.960
I’m actually just texting her right now.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Simon, why don’t you just kick it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:36.960 --> 01:19:42.880
off? Because I mean, in a sense, you did, I mean,&nbsp;
two or three years ago, you did the five year&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:43.600 --> 01:19:48.240
forward plan for mental health and just set&nbsp;
the context while we try and find Claire.&nbsp;

01:19:48.240 --> 01:19:55.360
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Okay. Well, look, the context&nbsp;
is that when you measure the burden of illness&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:55.360 --> 01:20:00.240
and disability, mental health conditions represent&nbsp;
about a quarter and yet in terms of the share of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:00.960 --> 01:20:07.680
NHS funding and the treatment availability, it’s&nbsp;
less than half of that. And therefore, we made an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:07.680 --> 01:20:14.880
in-principle decision that we being NHS England,&nbsp;
that for the past six years, we would ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:14.880 --> 01:20:21.200
that the share of the NHS’ budget going on mental&nbsp;
health would go up faster each year than for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:21.200 --> 01:20:26.800
rest of the services the NHS was providing. Again,&nbsp;
as a means to an end. The question then is how do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:26.800 --> 01:20:31.840
you channel extra resource to deal with some of&nbsp;
the unmet need, and frankly, Claire, and Tim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:31.840 --> 01:20:36.320
and the original group of stakeholders, patient&nbsp;
representatives, service users who put together&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:36.320 --> 01:20:41.200
the blueprint have done a fantastic job of&nbsp;
dealing with some of the most acute service gaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:41.200 --> 01:20:46.000
that exist there, whether it’s on perinatal mental&nbsp;
health, whether it’s expanding talking therapies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:46.560 --> 01:20:52.080
whether it’s getting to grips with&nbsp;
some of the adult secure services.&nbsp;

01:20:52.080 --> 01:20:56.160
So I think, as you’ll hear, and I can see Claire&nbsp;
now so I’m going to step off very shortly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:56.800 --> 01:21:00.640
that journey for the last four or five&nbsp;
years has actually been one of concrete&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:00.640 --> 01:21:05.120
and practical achievement, and there is a sense&nbsp;
of momentum across the mental health services.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:06.000 --> 01:21:12.640
But it is by no means mission accomplished, not&nbsp;
least because as a result of the pandemic, we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:12.640 --> 01:21:18.800
seeing very increased presentations for children&nbsp;
and young people services, eating disorders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:18.800 --> 01:21:24.400
and a range of other psychological consequences,&nbsp;
including for people with long COVID who need&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:24.400 --> 01:21:32.400
multidisciplinary responses. And so there is a&nbsp;
big job of work ahead that has only been frankly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:32.400 --> 01:21:38.720
intensified as a result of what the pandemic has&nbsp;
brought about. So huge amount achieved, a lot more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:38.720 --> 01:21:45.520
to do would be my prologue to Claire and Tim’s&nbsp;
presentation. Now they’ve joined, I’ll hand over.&nbsp;

01:21:45.520 --> 01:21:50.800
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Thank you. And thank you so&nbsp;
much, everybody, I’ve been on the board meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:50.800 --> 01:21:57.680
throughout and when I came to take myself off&nbsp;
mute, the whole lot froze. So I’ve logged out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:57.680 --> 01:22:04.720
logged back in again. And I’m hugely relieved and&nbsp;
delighted to have the opportunity along with Tim&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:04.720 --> 01:22:11.680
Kendall, to update the board on progress against&nbsp;
mental health. So I’m in now and panic over.&nbsp;

01:22:12.800 --> 01:22:17.760
Look, a few things, and Simon has really&nbsp;
set the scene beautifully. We set up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:18.320 --> 01:22:22.640
Simon Stevens set up the mental&nbsp;
health task force back in 2015&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:23.200 --> 01:22:30.640
because it was obvious that we the NHS actually&nbsp;
were not getting the focus, the investment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:30.640 --> 01:22:38.480
the thought, and the drive to really develop the&nbsp;
mental health services that our patients deserved.&nbsp;

01:22:38.480 --> 01:22:45.440
That task force, which had some 30,000 bits&nbsp;
of input from professionals, the public and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:46.400 --> 01:22:52.640
stakeholders and others from across the country,&nbsp;
set about establishing the five year forward view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:52.640 --> 01:23:00.880
which in turn actually fed into more recently&nbsp;
the long-term plan. We are five years into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:01.440 --> 01:23:08.720
implementing an evidence based, highly&nbsp;
focused and targeted mental health programme.&nbsp;

01:23:09.600 --> 01:23:16.000
The challenge in talking about that programme in&nbsp;
one slot is we probably would never attempt to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:16.000 --> 01:23:22.000
talk about all of physical health in one slot. I’m&nbsp;
really keen to remind the board that when we talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:22.000 --> 01:23:27.520
about mental health, we’re talking about a range&nbsp;
of illnesses and disorders. And we are talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:27.520 --> 01:23:35.360
about those services and these developments&nbsp;
across the age range literally from preconception&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:35.920 --> 01:23:39.760
if we’re talking about our specialist&nbsp;
perinatal mental health services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:39.760 --> 01:23:46.480
and the first two years of life, all the way&nbsp;
through to adults of working age and older people.&nbsp;

01:23:46.480 --> 01:23:54.240
We’re talking about a range of disorders. Simon&nbsp;
has mentioned some but from psychosis through to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:54.240 --> 01:24:02.880
bipolar disorders, or eating disorders and a whole&nbsp;
range of other issues. So it’s a big programme.&nbsp;

01:24:02.880 --> 01:24:10.800
To remind the board, the long-term plan seeks&nbsp;
to ensure that we will invest £2.3 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:11.360 --> 01:24:16.320
a year additionally, in mental&nbsp;
health services by 2023/24,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:16.320 --> 01:24:21.680
and that’s real term, so it will be uplifted&nbsp;
for growth. And that will enable us to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:22.320 --> 01:24:28.080
2 million more patients a year across the&nbsp;
pathway, some of which I’ve mentioned.&nbsp;

01:24:29.040 --> 01:24:34.560
It’s also true to say that that money has&nbsp;
been ring fenced, we call it the mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:34.560 --> 01:24:41.360
investment standard. And we’ve been clear&nbsp;
that spending on mental health to 2023/24&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:41.360 --> 01:24:48.800
needs to grow at a faster rate than general&nbsp;
spending on the NHS. And within that, spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:48.800 --> 01:24:54.320
on children and young people’s mental health&nbsp;
services needs to grow at a faster rate still.&nbsp;

01:24:54.320 --> 01:24:59.680
So we’ve built those principles&nbsp;
into absolutely all of our planning.&nbsp;

01:25:00.400 --> 01:25:05.120
It’s also true to say that we’ve worked really&nbsp;
hard with some experts from across the globe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:05.120 --> 01:25:09.360
across the country and very much Professor&nbsp;
Kendall’s led this work with other&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:09.360 --> 01:25:15.600
great colleagues and people with lived experience.&nbsp;
We built our programmes on the strongest available&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:15.600 --> 01:25:20.400
evidence base. So we’ve really tried to&nbsp;
make sure we’re investing this money wisely.&nbsp;

01:25:23.520 --> 01:25:28.480
And as part of that, and I’m going to come on just&nbsp;
very briefly talk about some of the specifics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:28.480 --> 01:25:33.120
As part of that we’ve tried not just to think&nbsp;
about doing more in the evidence base, but to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:33.120 --> 01:25:39.680
also think about transformation in its truest&nbsp;
sense. I do sometimes think the NHS just never&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:39.680 --> 01:25:45.120
changes anything; we have to transform everything.&nbsp;
Well, I think for the mental health programme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:45.120 --> 01:25:50.480
that transformation word is absolutely true.&nbsp;
And there are several things in the long-term&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:50.480 --> 01:25:55.520
plan that are important to how we do things&nbsp;
differently. I would point to just a couple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:56.080 --> 01:26:01.280
but things like can we stop thinking that&nbsp;
children and young people services cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:01.280 --> 01:26:08.320
off at age 18? And we’ve had strong support&nbsp;
for a 0 to 25 set of thinking and pathways.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:08.320 --> 01:26:15.280
And I can see nods there, or and COVID&nbsp;
has helped with this in a bizarre way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:15.280 --> 01:26:23.680
can we use digital and other applications&nbsp;
much more innovatively to put people more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:23.680 --> 01:26:28.480
in control of their own health and care,&nbsp;
but also to make sure we join it up.&nbsp;

01:26:29.040 --> 01:26:35.840
So that’s a sort of very high-level outline of&nbsp;
what we’re trying to do in the long-term plan.&nbsp;

01:26:37.040 --> 01:26:43.760
We are tackling a pre-existing treatment gap&nbsp;
for the reasons I’ve said, and the reason that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:43.760 --> 01:26:50.000
Simon actually set up the task force back&nbsp;
in 2015, the very obvious treatment gap. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:50.000 --> 01:26:57.120
everything we’re doing in this space is seeking&nbsp;
to reduce that treatment gap. It’s job begun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:57.120 --> 01:27:04.160
and even by 2023/24, it won’t be job done. But it&nbsp;
seems to us it’s really important to acknowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:04.160 --> 01:27:08.800
and celebrate the progress whilst being&nbsp;
honest about what ought to be done.&nbsp;

01:27:09.760 --> 01:27:15.760
Secondly, I just want to say that we&nbsp;
achieve nothing without our partners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:17.280 --> 01:27:23.760
particularly across the third sector in local&nbsp;
government, in education, in employment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:23.760 --> 01:27:30.080
and elsewhere, housing, and so on, and so forth.&nbsp;
Hugely important. Those partners are incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:30.080 --> 01:27:36.080
important. And we’ve developed a lot of our plan&nbsp;
with them. And we talk a lot with them about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:37.120 --> 01:27:44.640
prevention, and mental wellbeing and assessment&nbsp;
and treatment of mental illness and how we bridge&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:44.640 --> 01:27:50.480
the two. And the NHS does its job while&nbsp;
supporting other partners to do theirs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:51.360 --> 01:27:54.880
So I think the partnership&nbsp;
piece is incredibly important.&nbsp;

01:27:55.440 --> 01:28:02.320
I’ve put in the paper, but there are so many more&nbsp;
examples of where five years ago we had nothing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:03.040 --> 01:28:11.200
or very little in certain patient&nbsp;
pathways. Now we can say for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:11.200 --> 01:28:18.240
that specialist perinatal services exist right&nbsp;
across the country. They didn’t five years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:18.880 --> 01:28:23.520
We now have what’s widely considered to&nbsp;
be the world’s finest talking therapist&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:24.160 --> 01:28:29.600
programme and many other countries come from&nbsp;
across the globe to see what we’re doing there.&nbsp;

01:28:29.600 --> 01:28:33.600
We’ve worked hard to establish&nbsp;
24/7 crisis services.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:34.240 --> 01:28:42.320
All of our A&amp;E teams now have specialist mental&nbsp;
health input. They didn’t five years ago. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:42.320 --> 01:28:49.040
a few years ago got our first access and waiting&nbsp;
time standards for mental health about 20 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:49.040 --> 01:28:54.720
after the rest of the NHS. And I know we have to&nbsp;
be careful what we wish for. But it seemed to us&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:54.720 --> 01:29:01.360
incredibly important that we were able to see&nbsp;
people sooner. And we’re on that journey, still.&nbsp;

01:29:01.360 --> 01:29:04.640
We know there’s more to be done around&nbsp;
the clinical review of standards.&nbsp;

01:29:05.760 --> 01:29:12.240
Our assessment for memory assessment clinics&nbsp;
across the country, and the standards we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:12.240 --> 01:29:20.640
set there have been a huge achievement. We opened&nbsp;
the NHS first gambling addiction clinic recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:20.640 --> 01:29:26.640
and under that for children and young people&nbsp;
as well. So there are lots of firsts or lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:26.640 --> 01:29:33.120
of extensions of what we’ve been doing. We’ve&nbsp;
during the period seen approximately a 20%&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:33.120 --> 01:29:40.560
growth in our investment, and about a 14500&nbsp;
growth in our workforce and we work closely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:40.560 --> 01:29:47.440
with our colleagues at Health Education England,&nbsp;
and workforce I would say alongside the digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:47.440 --> 01:29:53.040
and capital are the three huge enablers&nbsp;
to our programme but above all workforce.&nbsp;

01:29:54.720 --> 01:30:00.720
During the COVID period, mental health&nbsp;
services absolutely maintained their focus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:01.360 --> 01:30:09.200
And we’re really busily working. We saw a tail&nbsp;
off in, for example, the UEC pathway attendances&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:09.200 --> 01:30:17.280
in A&amp;E, we’re really back to now pre COVID&nbsp;
levels and busier than ever before. But we also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:17.280 --> 01:30:24.560
for example, began to work around long COVID,&nbsp;
and what the psychological treatments for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:24.560 --> 01:30:30.400
people experiencing long COVID need to be. And&nbsp;
I know Professor David Clarke ran, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:30.400 --> 01:30:36.960
training for our IR therapists where we have&nbsp;
more than 3000 therapists on a training programme&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:36.960 --> 01:30:42.480
looking at interventions for long COVID.
And we heard Prerana earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:42.480 --> 01:30:48.400
talking about the mental health support hubs for&nbsp;
staff that have opened across the country during&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:48.400 --> 01:30:53.200
the last few months, and they have very much&nbsp;
been led and provided by mental health staff.&nbsp;

01:30:54.560 --> 01:30:59.760
All of our work has been co-produced by patients&nbsp;
and their families and people with lived&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:59.760 --> 01:31:05.040
experience, we’re incredibly grateful for them.&nbsp;
We’ve worked with government and others to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:05.040 --> 01:31:11.564
to lay down the foundations for a new Mental&nbsp;
Health Act that’s more rights based and risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:11.564 --> 01:31:17.199
based. And that’s incredibly exciting. And I know&nbsp;
Tim Kendall will be able to talk to that as well.&nbsp;

01:31:17.920 --> 01:31:24.080
But what are we doing next? As Simon has&nbsp;
said, we know we’re seeing increased demand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:24.800 --> 01:31:30.080
So despite all of these achievements, we will&nbsp;
still hear regularly about people who struggled&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:30.080 --> 01:31:37.280
to access service or longer waiting times.&nbsp;
So we know priority number one is to continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:37.280 --> 01:31:43.600
to deliver our promises in the long-term plan.&nbsp;
We’ve evaluated them again, and reviewed them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:43.600 --> 01:31:50.000
to make sure that we pivot them for a world of&nbsp;
living with COVID. And they hold very much true.&nbsp;

01:31:50.800 --> 01:31:56.800
We as part of the children and young people’s&nbsp;
plan, we are investing more in our eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:56.800 --> 01:32:02.320
disorder services, but also a very exciting&nbsp;
step forward, the mental health support teams&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:02.320 --> 01:32:09.360
in schools, which are a vital bridge between&nbsp;
education, early intervention and caring for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:09.360 --> 01:32:13.120
treating, identifying and treating&nbsp;
those youngsters with mild to moderate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:15.120 --> 01:32:20.720
mental health problems, and a bridging to&nbsp;
specialist CAHMS. So for example, those services&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:21.280 --> 01:32:26.720
are rolling out across schools, we continue&nbsp;
to thunder with the implementation throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:26.720 --> 01:32:31.920
the year of COVID, making sure that we were&nbsp;
recruiting, training the therapists to work in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:31.920 --> 01:32:37.280
those services. And we, I think, if anything,&nbsp;
we were disrupted by maybe six weeks, no more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:37.280 --> 01:32:45.600
than that, we’ve really kept the pace up on that.
By 2023/24. If we continue with current momentum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:45.600 --> 01:32:54.080
we’ll have sufficient mental health support&nbsp;
therapists to cover 47% of schools nationally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:54.080 --> 01:32:58.640
So we do need to consider and we’re working&nbsp;
with government and others on can we go further,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:58.640 --> 01:33:04.000
faster, but that’s a key plank of what we&nbsp;
need to do. So really make sure we implement&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:04.000 --> 01:33:12.240
the long-term plan, make sure we consolidate the&nbsp;
learning from digital, we move so much of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:12.240 --> 01:33:21.840
therapy online and we’re actively taking views&nbsp;
from patients about effectiveness and outcomes.&nbsp;

01:33:22.480 --> 01:33:26.240
We know that workforce needs to&nbsp;
continue to be a major focus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:26.880 --> 01:33:32.320
And we will continue to review standards.
I do just want to say before handing over to Tim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:33.120 --> 01:33:38.240
there are some thank yous due here. And I&nbsp;
have to take this opportunity to make them if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:38.240 --> 01:33:45.600
you’ll forgive me. Our workforce have been quite&nbsp;
incredible, including the volunteer workforce.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:45.600 --> 01:33:51.200
There’s been a major focus on vaccination&nbsp;
and keeping people with mental illness safe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:51.200 --> 01:33:56.160
but also everything else connected with staying&nbsp;
in touch with people throughout what’s been an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:56.160 --> 01:34:01.200
incredible period. Our workforce have&nbsp;
been phenomenal. So thank you to them.&nbsp;

01:34:01.200 --> 01:34:08.320
Thank you to our partners who likewise have really&nbsp;
stepped into the sense of an emergency during&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:09.440 --> 01:34:13.680
COVID. And we’ve worked so amazingly&nbsp;
well together. They’ve met me every&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:13.680 --> 01:34:18.640
Friday morning to talk about how we pivot&nbsp;
our plans and their plans and what we do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:18.640 --> 01:34:25.040
next. So we’re terribly grateful for that.
My final thanks has to go to Simon Stevens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:25.040 --> 01:34:31.840
which he will dislike intensely, but I’m doing&nbsp;
it anyway, because I have the microphone. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:32.640 --> 01:34:40.480
without doubt your backing to improve mental&nbsp;
health services, extend mental health services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:40.480 --> 01:34:45.600
and ensure that the money gets through,&nbsp;
including independent audits to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:45.600 --> 01:34:50.960
sure that when we say the money has grown,&nbsp;
it’s independently audited, including when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:50.960 --> 01:34:58.640
we’ve had other pressures on the NHS budget, your&nbsp;
steadfast determination to ensure that we the NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:59.200 --> 01:35:06.560
absolutely implement these promises and go further&nbsp;
faster wherever we possibly can, I think has given&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:06.560 --> 01:35:12.720
hope to our workforce, who really believe&nbsp;
that the money is flowing. I can see in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:12.720 --> 01:35:18.480
trusts across the country, the growth is there&nbsp;
this year, people are busily making their plans.&nbsp;

01:35:18.480 --> 01:35:22.880
But more importantly than that, Simon,&nbsp;
I think your commitment to mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:23.520 --> 01:35:28.800
has underpinned what every civilised&nbsp;
modern health service across the globe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:28.800 --> 01:35:33.840
needs to tackle and embrace, which&nbsp;
is a sense of genuinely equal access,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:34.480 --> 01:35:42.000
genuinely equal investment and genuinely equal&nbsp;
value. So you will be missed and you are very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:42.000 --> 01:35:48.640
very big shoes to fill. Thank you. Tim Kendall&nbsp;
may well want to say something a bit about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:48.640 --> 01:35:55.840
eating disorders in particular, and one or two&nbsp;
other clinical presentations that we’re seeing.&nbsp;

01:35:56.720 --> 01:36:02.320
&gt;&gt;Tim Kendall: Very happy to, but I’m just&nbsp;
thinking that we’ll run out of time. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:02.320 --> 01:36:07.120
David, did you want me to say any more about?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I think, Claire, thank you very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:07.120 --> 01:36:11.200
much for that. And just echo what you said about&nbsp;
Simon’s commitment to mental health has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:11.200 --> 01:36:16.240
absolutely essential throughout this time. It’s&nbsp;
so easy to put it on the back burner, frankly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:16.240 --> 01:36:21.680
when there’s cancer and other you know, other&nbsp;
more eye-catching issues out there. But Tim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:21.680 --> 01:36:25.600
just quickly on, and then I’ll bring David&nbsp;
Roberts in, just quickly, maybe on eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:25.600 --> 01:36:30.720
orders in particular, just give the board a&nbsp;
bit of a flavour, because that’s very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:30.720 --> 01:36:35.760
a big issue at the moment, as we know.
&gt;&gt;Tim Kendall: Well, the impact of COVID&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:35.760 --> 01:36:41.680
has been probably the worst for people with&nbsp;
existing mental health problems. But in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:41.680 --> 01:36:48.400
of new mental health problems, eating disorders&nbsp;
I think is probably the one that stands out. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:49.680 --> 01:36:57.040
over the, just before COVID, we had got a&nbsp;
waiting time on eating disorders in young people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:57.040 --> 01:37:02.960
children and young people, and we were on&nbsp;
course, about 87% of young people were seen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:03.600 --> 01:37:10.640
if it was an urgent referral, treatment&nbsp;
was started within a week. Now, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:11.440 --> 01:37:20.560
we’re on course to get to 95%. But the numbers of&nbsp;
young people being taken into urgent treatment has&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:20.560 --> 01:37:29.920
gone up by about 120%. So that has knocked us&nbsp;
off course for getting to our 95%, I’m afraid.&nbsp;

01:37:30.960 --> 01:37:36.240
Now, you know, we have got plans, and&nbsp;
we are using some of the £500 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:36.960 --> 01:37:43.760
for this year that will be focused very&nbsp;
much on enhancing our response to in eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:43.760 --> 01:37:53.280
disorders. But it remains a real concern. And&nbsp;
the same applies to adults too. So big concern.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:57.120 --> 01:37:58.560
Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:58.560 --> 01:38:01.040
Tim. We’re going to, I’m sure we’ll come back&nbsp;
to you in a minute. But David, David Roberts.&nbsp;

01:38:01.040 --> 01:38:04.800
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Well, we might come back&nbsp;
to him quicker than he thought because this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:04.800 --> 01:38:10.400
is precisely the area that I was interested&nbsp;
in. So the first thing I’d like to say is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:11.760 --> 01:38:17.280
when I started out, I chaired the commissioning&nbsp;
committee and Claire used to come along fairly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:17.280 --> 01:38:24.400
frequently. And the progress that has been made in&nbsp;
that time is astonishing. And therefore alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:24.400 --> 01:38:33.360
the commitments of the team, of Simon and crew,&nbsp;
the scale and support and willingness to crash&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:33.360 --> 01:38:38.240
through any barriers that get in the way that&nbsp;
Claire has showed and the team has been excellent.&nbsp;

01:38:39.120 --> 01:38:44.560
I guess I remember, you know, the conversations&nbsp;
were all about workforce in a lot of ways along&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:44.560 --> 01:38:51.840
with resources. And we’re still looking at a&nbsp;
target to be able to get to 100% of children&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:51.840 --> 01:38:59.680
and young adults by 2028, which is seven years&nbsp;
away. And the world has changed a bit since then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:59.680 --> 01:39:05.920
I think in two or three sort of dimensions.
One is, is exactly what Tim was saying. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:05.920 --> 01:39:11.040
I’ve seen it upfront close and personal within&nbsp;
my broader family, Tim about exactly what you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:11.040 --> 01:39:20.080
saying. And the consequences of that which&nbsp;
are truly awful. And desperate cries for help.&nbsp;

01:39:20.640 --> 01:39:26.720
And then the second thing, of course, is the, and&nbsp;
that explosion of demand. And the second thing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:26.720 --> 01:39:35.200
the digital and data sort of revolution. And you&nbsp;
know, I see in my commercial world how powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:35.200 --> 01:39:44.400
some of these data, digital tools can be to help&nbsp;
people and get help early and self-help early.&nbsp;

01:39:44.960 --> 01:39:49.360
And I guess if I put those three things&nbsp;
together, sort of explosion of demand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:50.320 --> 01:39:56.400
workforce and still a long way being able to meet&nbsp;
the demand that serve in front of us never mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:56.400 --> 01:40:02.560
the acceleration of demand and then the use of&nbsp;
digital tools, whether there is any way and what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:02.560 --> 01:40:08.400
we would have to believe to change that trajectory&nbsp;
to get desperately needed help to not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:09.280 --> 01:40:13.840
children, young adults, but particularly&nbsp;
children, young adults, by leveraging some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:13.840 --> 01:40:18.560
of the new tools that are available and&nbsp;
Tim and Claire, that’s not to criticise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:18.560 --> 01:40:24.880
that’s to support and to say, can we do more?
&gt;&gt;Tim Kendall: I think in terms of the digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:24.880 --> 01:40:33.920
tools, so, for example, having digitalised&nbsp;
therapies, that kind of thing. I think that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:33.920 --> 01:40:39.440
is a huge future in this, but we’ve got a problem,&nbsp;
which is that the evidence base is lacking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:41.040 --> 01:40:49.200
Now, having said that, using video&nbsp;
methods of delivering therapy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:49.200 --> 01:40:54.000
there’s very good evidence around&nbsp;
that. And it probably certainly for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:56.640 --> 01:41:02.400
areas like anxiety, and depression,&nbsp;
and so on, the evidence for video&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:02.400 --> 01:41:07.920
delivered therapies is pretty good.
And there are some groups who really do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:08.800 --> 01:41:17.040
take to video methods. So for example, in&nbsp;
autism, and particularly high functioning autism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:17.040 --> 01:41:28.880
that these kids are very, very, very much happier&nbsp;
doing video at their own home than they are doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:28.880 --> 01:41:36.320
face to face and going into public settings. So&nbsp;
I think we’re in a position now where we’ve got&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:37.520 --> 01:41:45.840
significant growth in using video methods and we&nbsp;
are looking at digital methods alongside that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:46.560 --> 01:41:50.880
and I’m sure it is the right way to go.
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: And additionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:50.880 --> 01:41:57.120
I think we’ve learned a great deal. We’ve moved&nbsp;
very fast around the development of mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:57.120 --> 01:42:02.720
support teams in schools, we’ve literally gone&nbsp;
from writing a curriculum, ensuring it embeds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:02.720 --> 01:42:10.320
the evidence base, to commissioning universities,&nbsp;
to train graduates to become mental health support&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:10.320 --> 01:42:18.240
therapists in schools. And what we’ve learned,&nbsp;
actually, is that for every post we advertise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:19.360 --> 01:42:23.040
it’s hugely oversubscribed.
I think there is a bit of a mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:23.040 --> 01:42:28.960
health bounce going on. I think people are more&nbsp;
thoughtful society is more thoughtful about mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:28.960 --> 01:42:34.880
health now than it was before, it talks about it&nbsp;
more. We heard that a lot. So these new roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:34.880 --> 01:42:41.200
more peer support workers, we’ve seen thanks to&nbsp;
Ruth and colleagues and colleagues at HEE we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:41.200 --> 01:42:48.160
seen a big increase in people applying to become&nbsp;
mental health nurses. These are all roles that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:48.160 --> 01:42:58.560
can train relatively quickly. So I am hopeful&nbsp;
that we can, if we are absolutely resolute and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:58.560 --> 01:43:04.240
determined, bring the workforce in that we need.&nbsp;
We’ve grown the CAHMS workforce of children, young&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:04.240 --> 01:43:14.720
people’s workforce, by some 22%, from about 8500&nbsp;
to nearly 11,000 in the last two or three years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:14.720 --> 01:43:21.760
And so it’s hard. It’s really hard. But I think we&nbsp;
have to be determined, new roles, use of digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:21.760 --> 01:43:27.440
be really creative. And of course, it’s not just&nbsp;
about recruitment, it’s about the retention piece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:27.440 --> 01:43:31.680
as well, which we’re very, very focused on&nbsp;
but who wouldn’t want to come and work in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:31.680 --> 01:43:37.280
mental health? It’s got so many fabulous jobs.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: And just very quickly, David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:37.840 --> 01:43:42.720
one of the observations I think we’ve seen within&nbsp;
the health service, I think we’ve absolutely seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:42.720 --> 01:43:49.680
it in broader commercial world, is that if you if&nbsp;
you open up flexibility, and you can allow people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:49.680 --> 01:43:56.080
to work in different ways, and you can use digital&nbsp;
tools, as Tim just said, then suddenly you open up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:56.080 --> 01:44:02.240
a workforce that might otherwise not have been&nbsp;
there. And I’d really encourage you to push hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:02.240 --> 01:44:09.520
on that and really test the evidence base really&nbsp;
hard and see what, because there’s a real dilemma&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:09.520 --> 01:44:16.000
isn’t there between having perfect evidence, but&nbsp;
then having an unmet need of how those two work&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:16.000 --> 01:44:19.440
together and then I’ll be quiet. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: And may I add just one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:19.440 --> 01:44:23.360
other thing on workforce, which is we&nbsp;
do need a long-term workforce plan. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:23.360 --> 01:44:28.720
of the things that has helped us with&nbsp;
the mental health plan is it’s been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:29.680 --> 01:44:34.080
five-year plan. We’ve known what investments&nbsp;
come in; we’ve been able to work with HEE.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:34.720 --> 01:44:43.440
But it we absolutely must have that long term&nbsp;
investment to enable us to plan with certainty&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:43.440 --> 01:44:49.920
and with some innovation behind it as well.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Claire, look, thank you and Tim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:49.920 --> 01:44:56.240
very much. I know both of you are wholly&nbsp;
committed to this programme. It is so important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:56.240 --> 01:45:02.320
I’m guessing that pretty much everybody on the&nbsp;
board have been touched in some way or another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:02.320 --> 01:45:10.160
you know, friends, families, whatever&nbsp;
by mental health. I mean, it is a hugely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:10.160 --> 01:45:15.040
important area. And you know, we are still&nbsp;
only touching the surface of it in many&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:15.040 --> 01:45:19.520
areas as we know, but the work you’re doing&nbsp;
is fantastic, Claire and, and Tim to you as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:19.520 --> 01:45:24.400
well. You’re working with some of the most&nbsp;
vulnerable people in Sheffield. I know, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:24.960 --> 01:45:30.480
really, really important. So thank you very much.
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Thank you chair and I forgot to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:30.480 --> 01:45:35.440
thank one group. Well, firstly, your leadership&nbsp;
around mental health is much appreciated, too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:35.440 --> 01:45:42.240
But many people have said to me, not of the NHS,&nbsp;
critics, sometimes, of the NHS, campaigners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:42.880 --> 01:45:49.040
NHS England and Improvement have the best&nbsp;
national mental health team it has ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:49.040 --> 01:45:55.120
had. And I have to say, as a chief exec, a&nbsp;
nurse of 38 years working in mental health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:55.680 --> 01:46:00.080
I wholly endorse that view and&nbsp;
would like to thank my team&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:00.080 --> 01:46:06.800
and my colleagues, they are amazing. So thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: You must have meant 28 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:06.800 --> 01:46:10.560
not 38 years. Surely, Claire?
&gt;&gt;Claire Murdoch: Oh, I’m going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:10.560 --> 01:46:15.120
to join this board meeting every month&nbsp;
if I might going forward, Lord Prior.&nbsp;

01:46:15.120 --> 01:46:24.000
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Anyway. Thank you very much. And&nbsp;
Tim, for coming. Thank you. So can we move on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:24.000 --> 01:46:31.680
then to the health and social care bill, Ian?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you, David. I think it’s 28&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:31.680 --> 01:46:38.560
years since I first started working in health.&nbsp;
Look, I wanted to bring you a brief update today&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:38.560 --> 01:46:44.800
on legislation. And that’s not because legislative&nbsp;
reform in the NHS is going to be the main driver&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:44.800 --> 01:46:51.040
of effecting service transformation. We know it&nbsp;
isn’t, but it is an incredibly important way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:51.040 --> 01:46:58.480
cutting out things that stop frontline services&nbsp;
being able to do what they’d ideally like to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:59.840 --> 01:47:05.520
and avoid some of the elaborate workarounds,&nbsp;
whether that’s local planning of services or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:05.520 --> 01:47:12.080
joint working across organisational boundaries.
In 2018, the government asked the NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:12.080 --> 01:47:17.440
itself to make its own recommendations&nbsp;
on primary legislative reform. We took&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:17.440 --> 01:47:24.720
that request very seriously. We ran a massive&nbsp;
public engagement exercise with the NHS. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:24.720 --> 01:47:31.840
generated a clear consensus and strong support&nbsp;
for the set of proposals that our boards warmly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:31.840 --> 01:47:39.760
endorsed in September 2019. And then last year,&nbsp;
we additionally set out options for the statutory&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:39.760 --> 01:47:46.240
form of ICS’s, ran a second engagement exercise&nbsp;
and made further recommendations to government.&nbsp;

01:47:47.120 --> 01:47:53.440
I believe it was the clarity and consistency&nbsp;
of the NHS’s own views that made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:53.440 --> 01:47:58.560
easy for the government then to accept and&nbsp;
adopt all of these sets of recommendations&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:58.560 --> 01:48:06.560
in its February 2021 white paper.
As Simon said in his opening remarks today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:06.560 --> 01:48:12.880
the health and social care bill is now expected&nbsp;
to be published very shortly and brought forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:12.880 --> 01:48:18.800
for its second reading in the House of Commons&nbsp;
before the summer recess. It’s a major piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:18.800 --> 01:48:25.680
legislation, it will be the first since the health&nbsp;
and social care act 2012. Inevitably, there will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:25.680 --> 01:48:30.560
be a lot of external interest and the health&nbsp;
and social care select committee has already&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:30.560 --> 01:48:35.360
considered legislation on multiple occasions,&nbsp;
including publishing report earlier this year.&nbsp;

01:48:36.560 --> 01:48:44.080
And today, I highlight three points in particular.&nbsp;
So first, amongst the bill’s wider provisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:44.080 --> 01:48:49.680
we look forward to those clauses that give effect&nbsp;
to the NHS’s own recommendations, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:49.680 --> 01:48:57.840
for us, the abolition of the TDA and monitor and&nbsp;
transfer of NHSI duties and powers to NHS England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:59.360 --> 01:49:02.800
Secondly, we heard loud and clear from the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:03.920 --> 01:49:09.160
please ensure that the bill is sufficiently&nbsp;
permissive and flexible for ICSs&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:10.400 --> 01:49:13.840
so that arrangements can be&nbsp;
tailored to our local circumstances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:14.800 --> 01:49:22.560
And thirdly, we really welcome the government’s&nbsp;
commitment to supporting ICSs being able to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:22.560 --> 01:49:31.440
statutory form from April 2022. And this date will&nbsp;
be very important to avoid NHS operational delays.&nbsp;

01:49:31.440 --> 01:49:37.120
And so David, I invite the board to note&nbsp;
that the publication of draft health and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:37.120 --> 01:49:41.840
social care bill and its first reading&nbsp;
in Parliament is now imminent. Thank you.&nbsp;

01:49:43.840 --> 01:49:46.960
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Ian, and&nbsp;
you’ve offered I think a teaching to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:46.960 --> 01:49:54.640
board if they’d like a more detailed look at&nbsp;
it in the next 10 days or so. Anyone on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:54.640 --> 01:49:58.480
board have any comments they want to make to&nbsp;
Ian now or can we take that sort of offline?&nbsp;

01:50:00.480 --> 01:50:05.360
Excellent. Thank you all very&nbsp;
much. And so is Nick Watts with us?&nbsp;

01:50:05.360 --> 01:50:09.840
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: Yeah. Here.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Oh, hello, Nick. Welcome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:11.120 --> 01:50:15.920
So look, we’ll move on to the net zero&nbsp;
paper that you’ve given us. Ian, do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:15.920 --> 01:50:20.200
just want to introduce it and introduce Nick?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: No, let’s go straight to Nick.&nbsp;

01:50:20.200 --> 01:50:24.560
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay, Nick, over to you.
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: Hi. Thank you very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:24.560 --> 01:50:29.280
very much. Thanks for having me today.&nbsp;
Thank you, I assume for reading the paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:32.160 --> 01:50:36.480
In October, six months, seven, eight months&nbsp;
ago, the NHS became the world’s first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:37.040 --> 01:50:42.240
healthcare system, the very first to commit&nbsp;
to net zero carbon emissions. By far the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:42.240 --> 01:50:50.640
most ambitious commitment out there, by far the&nbsp;
world leader in its response to climate change.&nbsp;

01:50:51.760 --> 01:50:57.040
We did this because we understand the profound&nbsp;
impacts of climate change on human health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:57.040 --> 01:51:04.480
we understand that as 5-6% of the public sector’s&nbsp;
emissions, sorry, 5-6% of the country’s emissions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:04.480 --> 01:51:10.800
29% of the public sector’s emissions, we have&nbsp;
a responsibility to act. But most importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:10.800 --> 01:51:16.160
I think, because over a decade of responding&nbsp;
to climate change has taught us that it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:16.160 --> 01:51:22.640
actually perfectly aligned with good quality care,&nbsp;
sensible public health, with the long-term plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:22.640 --> 01:51:28.640
with the long-term priorities of the NHS.
It was met with some resounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:29.600 --> 01:51:33.840
support. The director general of the World Health&nbsp;
Organization, our colleagues across government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:35.360 --> 01:51:42.320
We now see the beginnings of health care&nbsp;
systems in the United States, Australia, Canada,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:42.320 --> 01:51:50.560
New Zealand, Scotland, Wales, starting to starting&nbsp;
to build up the infrastructure to do very similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:50.560 --> 01:51:57.840
things, deliver net-zero healthcare systems.
Since that moment, there has been an enormous&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:57.840 --> 01:52:03.760
amount of progress. That progress for the&nbsp;
most part has come from the passion of nurses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:03.760 --> 01:52:08.320
allied health professionals, doctors,&nbsp;
sustainability leads across the entire system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:09.520 --> 01:52:15.040
You may remember every year we go out and&nbsp;
we survey staff asking them around about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:15.040 --> 01:52:19.600
their attitudes on this issue. Nine out of 10&nbsp;
every year come back saying that they want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:19.600 --> 01:52:24.240
see the NHS act in a more environmentally&nbsp;
sustainable way because there was an oath&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:24.240 --> 01:52:29.840
that some of them took at the very start of their&nbsp;
career: First do no harm. It aligns perfectly.&nbsp;

01:52:31.680 --> 01:52:37.680
So on the emissions that we control directly,&nbsp;
and to remind you, the commitments there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:38.400 --> 01:52:44.480
net zero by 2040 for those direct emissions,&nbsp;
an 80% reduction target only seven years away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:45.600 --> 01:52:48.480
2028. There we have some good progress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:49.600 --> 01:52:55.520
Just in the last couple of months, over a quarter&nbsp;
of a billion, £260 million flowing into the NHS&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:55.520 --> 01:53:00.240
going to trusts across the country, every region,&nbsp;
from the public sector decarbonisation scheme.&nbsp;

01:53:01.280 --> 01:53:06.720
The emissions reductions from that as well as&nbsp;
our own investments, take that over the course&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:07.520 --> 01:53:14.000
of a decade, we are taking the equivalent of 2.9&nbsp;
million transatlantic flights out of the skies&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:14.000 --> 01:53:19.920
from New York to London. This is going into things&nbsp;
like improved building fabric, changing the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:19.920 --> 01:53:26.880
that we heat and we light our buildings. Sussex&nbsp;
Community Foundation Trust have already celebrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:26.880 --> 01:53:32.800
just the other day, a 42% reduction in their&nbsp;
own emissions, saving £10 million as they go.&nbsp;

01:53:35.360 --> 01:53:38.480
As well as our estate it changes&nbsp;
the way we deliver care. So we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:38.480 --> 01:53:43.680
been working closely with the Royal College of&nbsp;
Anaesthetists, the Association of Anaesthetists&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:43.680 --> 01:53:51.120
reducing the proportion and reliance on&nbsp;
heavily carbon polluting gases or desflurane&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:51.120 --> 01:53:57.520
with clinically equivalent alternatives available.&nbsp;
Desflurane, one bottle of that is responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:57.520 --> 01:54:05.760
about 353kg of coal, just one bottle. Over the&nbsp;
last year or so we’ve managed to reduce the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:05.760 --> 01:54:16.960
proportion of desflurane from 23% down to 10.3%&nbsp;
shifting to simple often cheaper alternatives.&nbsp;

01:54:17.920 --> 01:54:22.160
University Hospital Bristol Trust they have&nbsp;
led the way there, they think they have reduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:22.800 --> 01:54:26.480
purely from their work engaging with anaesthetists&nbsp;
across the system, they think they’ve reduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:27.760 --> 01:54:34.000
emissions by over 30,000kg every month. To put&nbsp;
that in context again the equivalent of 105,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:34.560 --> 01:54:37.760
miles taken off the road every&nbsp;
single month from one trust.&nbsp;

01:54:40.400 --> 01:54:44.960
The same can be said when you look at transport.&nbsp;
There is impressive work with the London Ambulance&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:44.960 --> 01:54:50.640
Service, Project Zero. The West Midlands Ambulance&nbsp;
Service developing zero emission ambulances. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:50.640 --> 01:54:54.800
aren’t just fun alternatives. These are the&nbsp;
future of ambulances. This is what legally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:54.800 --> 01:55:03.520
the next round of ambulances are going to have to&nbsp;
look like when we take that step into 2030 and hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:03.520 --> 01:55:07.920
the new requirements that you won’t be able to&nbsp;
purchase a new diesel or a new petrol vehicle&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:07.920 --> 01:55:11.840
from that moment onwards, and hence a&nbsp;
question of the NHS being ready for that.&nbsp;

01:55:13.680 --> 01:55:20.080
It extends as well though, Guys and St Thomas’s&nbsp;
only a couple of weeks ago announced the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:20.080 --> 01:55:24.720
riverboat service, replacing the trucks&nbsp;
that previously took waste from engine and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:27.040 --> 01:55:35.920
gave goods to the hospitals, 1500 miles off&nbsp;
the road per week, important in London because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:35.920 --> 01:55:39.280
of the air pollution consequences and the&nbsp;
dire health effects of that air pollution.&nbsp;

01:55:39.920 --> 01:55:45.680
And similarly, Oxford University Hospital,&nbsp;
25,000 deliveries of life saving chemotherapy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:45.680 --> 01:55:52.320
antibiotics, IV nutrition every single month,&nbsp;
delivered by bike since March. All these sorts&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:52.320 --> 01:55:56.640
of things, I think should give us confidence that&nbsp;
there is great energy, great passion out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:56.640 --> 01:56:03.600
system to reduce the emissions here. Those&nbsp;
are for the emissions we control directly.&nbsp;

01:56:04.720 --> 01:56:09.680
David, you’ll be very, very familiar with the&nbsp;
emissions that are much broader than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:09.680 --> 01:56:13.280
right? We’re good health care professionals,&nbsp;
we understand that health care extends beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:13.280 --> 01:56:20.240
the four walls of a hospital. And so we have our&nbsp;
carbon footprint plus commitment, 2045 for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:20.240 --> 01:56:26.880
an 80% reduction target by the mid-2030s, 2036.&nbsp;
We’ve been very, very clear within the decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:26.880 --> 01:56:31.280
the NHS will no longer purchase from those that&nbsp;
don’t meet or exceed our net zero commitments.&nbsp;

01:56:32.400 --> 01:56:40.400
That sounds dangerous, scary. It’s actually&nbsp;
very, very achievable. And in fact, today, David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:41.920 --> 01:56:45.920
we have the announcement of your new net zero&nbsp;
international leadership group you’re chairing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:45.920 --> 01:56:50.800
pulling together some of our closest friends.&nbsp;
Nigel Topping, the Prime Minister’s champion&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:51.520 --> 01:56:55.120
on climate change, leaders from the British&nbsp;
Medical Journal, from the Health Foundation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:55.840 --> 01:57:01.440
But chief executives as well from suppliers&nbsp;
that have demonstrated this is possible:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:01.440 --> 01:57:05.280
AstraZeneca, GSK, Biogen,&nbsp;
Johnson and Johnson, Unilever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:05.280 --> 01:57:10.720
Microsoft all with net-zero commitments&nbsp;
perfectly aligned with the NHS commitments.&nbsp;

01:57:13.440 --> 01:57:21.360
That’s where we are today. There’s plenty more to&nbsp;
do. Because this can’t just happen. It can’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:21.360 --> 01:57:26.320
exist at the national level. It has to permeate&nbsp;
through everything that the NHS does. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:27.040 --> 01:57:33.200
reaffirming our commitment to this letter heading&nbsp;
out today to every trust chief executive to every&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:33.200 --> 01:57:40.400
chair of every integrated care system, a new ask:&nbsp;
the development of a local net-zero strategy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:40.400 --> 01:57:45.600
a local green plan, with guidance and support to&nbsp;
help deliver that. But to help make this real,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:45.600 --> 01:57:53.040
where the rubber hits the road or the back wheel&nbsp;
hits the crack or whatever that may be. We will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:53.040 --> 01:57:59.520
see those out and returned back by the 31st of&nbsp;
March, coming just around the corner, accompanied&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:59.520 --> 01:58:05.920
by approval from the board of those organisations&nbsp;
and a new board level net-zero lead for each of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:05.920 --> 01:58:10.240
those trusts and each of those integrated care&nbsp;
systems an essential part of delivering this.&nbsp;

01:58:11.600 --> 01:58:14.880
That’s accompanied with a new&nbsp;
all staff engagement campaign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:14.880 --> 01:58:18.400
providing an opportunity to showcase&nbsp;
more and more of the kinds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:19.200 --> 01:58:23.440
work that I was just talking about. Because to&nbsp;
be honest, where the energy for this work is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:23.440 --> 01:58:27.360
it isn’t just at the national level, it is&nbsp;
everywhere, the whole way through the system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:28.480 --> 01:58:33.920
It’s accompanied with new capacity, new net&nbsp;
zero teams set up nationally and across every&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:33.920 --> 01:58:41.200
single region. Analytics that we will have ready&nbsp;
for the board by September 30th. The ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:41.200 --> 01:58:46.400
track carbon footprints not just at the national&nbsp;
level, but now at the regional level. Now the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:46.400 --> 01:58:52.160
level of each ICS. So that we can hold ourselves&nbsp;
to account for our collective commitments.&nbsp;

01:58:53.920 --> 01:58:56.240
And then the promise that we&nbsp;
will come back again and again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:57.200 --> 01:59:03.760
twice a year to the board. I’m tracking&nbsp;
progress, talking about what else we can do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:03.760 --> 01:59:09.680
And I bet you moving that date forward, not net&nbsp;
zero by 2040, I bet you we can get there quicker.&nbsp;

01:59:11.360 --> 01:59:15.440
So that’s it, it should be clear that this work&nbsp;
has existed for a very long time, but that an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:15.440 --> 01:59:21.440
enormous step change has taken place over the last&nbsp;
six months. Enormous step change. That is driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:21.440 --> 01:59:26.160
by those passionate individuals out there. It is&nbsp;
palpable everywhere you go. Everyone you talk to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:26.160 --> 01:59:33.440
never once have I been told since I showed up six&nbsp;
months ago, that now is not the time. Every single&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:33.440 --> 01:59:37.760
door you walk into you get pulled down and people&nbsp;
say how can I go further? How can I go faster?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:39.520 --> 01:59:42.080
Thank you very much.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, Nick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:42.080 --> 01:59:47.360
thank you very much for that. Look, you’ve brought&nbsp;
not just passion to this, but science and data&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:47.360 --> 01:59:53.760
to it as well, which is really important. And you&nbsp;
put over a very, very persuasive, compelling case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:53.760 --> 01:59:58.800
I’m just going to ask actually, Susan who’s one&nbsp;
of our non-exec directors, you know, who’s on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:58.800 --> 02:00:06.480
the board of Unilever where I think Unilever have&nbsp;
had net zero sustainability really at the heart of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:06.480 --> 02:00:11.840
the business for quite a few years. Susan, could&nbsp;
you give us a perspective of how you look at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:11.840 --> 02:00:15.840
issue on the board of Unilever?
&gt;&gt;Susan Kilsby:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:15.840 --> 02:00:21.040
Sure. And maybe even beyond Unilever,&nbsp;
I can come in a little more broadly on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:21.600 --> 02:00:25.120
how companies are looking at this. I&nbsp;
think it’s, this is a, first of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:25.120 --> 02:00:29.840
I wholeheartedly endorse what you’re doing.&nbsp;
And I think this is a really important effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:29.840 --> 02:00:37.600
and completely consistent with our goals at&nbsp;
the NHS. So I welcome all that you’re doing.&nbsp;

02:00:38.880 --> 02:00:45.040
You know, I think this is critically important,&nbsp;
not just in terms of what we do, but in the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:45.040 --> 02:00:50.320
we look at it at Unilever and others is, but also&nbsp;
how all of our partners operate and making sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:50.320 --> 02:00:55.840
that we’re partners with those people who are&nbsp;
consistent with, with the views that we have.&nbsp;

02:00:57.680 --> 02:01:02.560
I think one of the real challenges we&nbsp;
have is measurement and making sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:03.520 --> 02:01:11.040
we set goals and we measure them appropriately.&nbsp;
And that we give assurance to people who look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:11.040 --> 02:01:16.800
those numbers that we’re doing what we say we’re&nbsp;
doing because there are goalposts that can move&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:18.880 --> 02:01:25.440
quite significantly over the course of the life&nbsp;
of the metrics that we set. So I you know, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:25.440 --> 02:01:35.840
think what we’ve looked at are really metrics that&nbsp;
are measurable, and that are strategic. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:35.840 --> 02:01:41.600
those are the things that are really important.
So I think, looking at what the kinds of goalposts&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:41.600 --> 02:01:49.840
you’re setting, making sure that they’re important&nbsp;
to the NHS, whether it’s employees, patients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:50.640 --> 02:01:54.720
the full spectrum of all of our stakeholders,&nbsp;
and then making sure that it’s something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:54.720 --> 02:01:59.120
we can measure. But again, I applaud&nbsp;
you for what you’re doing. Well done.&nbsp;

02:01:59.120 --> 02:02:05.040
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yeah, I mean, Nick, you’ve&nbsp;
really made a huge difference in the six&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:05.040 --> 02:02:10.080
months that you’ve been doing this role.&nbsp;
Simon, did you want to sort of comment at all&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:10.080 --> 02:02:15.840
from your perspective of this?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: I don’t think so. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:15.840 --> 02:02:19.920
frankly, Nick is doing a brilliant job. He’s laid&nbsp;
out really well. The connections between health&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:19.920 --> 02:02:27.280
and contribution to net carbon reduction are&nbsp;
pretty clear. And this is entirely consistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:27.280 --> 02:02:33.360
with what the government overall is looking to do&nbsp;
even on questions like procurement. The government&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:33.360 --> 02:02:42.320
has announced within the last three weeks that&nbsp;
for the services that are bought by the taxpayer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:42.320 --> 02:02:47.840
which is £290 billion of procurement each year,&nbsp;
then the commitment on the part of supply chain&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:47.840 --> 02:02:52.240
partners to reaching our net zero targets&nbsp;
will be hardwired into the contract awards.&nbsp;

02:02:52.240 --> 02:02:56.560
So we’re doing what is entirely consistent with&nbsp;
the approach that the government’s taking more&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:56.560 --> 02:03:01.120
widely. And there will be significant&nbsp;
health payoffs from getting this right.&nbsp;

02:03:02.960 --> 02:03:06.720
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Munir, then Mike.
&gt;&gt;Munir Pirmohamed:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:06.720 --> 02:03:12.480
Thanks very much, David. And Nick, fantastic&nbsp;
progress in six months. Just want to ask you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:12.480 --> 02:03:17.360
specifically about anaesthesia and anaesthetic&nbsp;
gases. We’ve known about the global warming&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:17.360 --> 02:03:22.720
potential of these gases for many, many years.&nbsp;
It’s great to see that, you know, we’re reducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:22.720 --> 02:03:27.840
the use of those which are particularly able&nbsp;
to do this global warming effect and so on.&nbsp;

02:03:28.480 --> 02:03:34.240
What do you see is the future of anaesthesia&nbsp;
in terms as we move forward? And in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:34.240 --> 02:03:39.120
development of new anaesthetics? You know, how is&nbsp;
the anaesthetic field approaching this? It would&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:39.120 --> 02:03:45.280
be good to understand your perspective on this.
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:45.280 --> 02:03:51.440
It’s a good question. You’re right. We have known&nbsp;
about this for a very long time. To some extent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:54.640 --> 02:03:59.120
previously over previous decades, healthcare&nbsp;
systems have been exempt from many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:59.680 --> 02:04:03.600
sort of commitments on climate change. That has&nbsp;
changed. Very, very clearly that has changed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:04.480 --> 02:04:10.080
thankfully the NHS has been ready for that.
When it comes to anaesthetics, you have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:10.080 --> 02:04:16.480
few options. We’re either going to talk about&nbsp;
changing, changing what’s being prescribed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:16.480 --> 02:04:21.200
right? So the classic desflurane,&nbsp;
sevoflurane, isoflurane or indeed away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:22.080 --> 02:04:27.920
some of the volatile anaesthetic gases. You&nbsp;
can also talk then about lean anaesthetics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:27.920 --> 02:04:32.720
right which aligns again perfectly with&nbsp;
the direction the profession is heading.&nbsp;

02:04:35.520 --> 02:04:40.880
Importantly, though, it is not all a matter of&nbsp;
clinical choice. So there is also a lot that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:40.880 --> 02:04:46.720
can be done in the estate to reduce leakage, to&nbsp;
change the way that the anaesthetics are captured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:46.720 --> 02:04:50.080
that they are recycled, that they&nbsp;
are harvested. I should say there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:50.080 --> 02:04:54.800
been some successful innovation the NHS has led&nbsp;
specifically on the harvesting and recycling of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:55.440 --> 02:05:00.640
anaesthetic gases. It saves money for&nbsp;
the system, and it’s reinvested back&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:00.640 --> 02:05:06.880
into anaesthetic services. There’s a&nbsp;
lot that can be done there as well as,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:10.000 --> 02:05:15.680
as well as efforts probably slightly more distal&nbsp;
from the four walls of an operating theatre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:15.680 --> 02:05:21.200
which get a little closer to how a manufacturer&nbsp;
is producing that, right? So they add on closer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:21.200 --> 02:05:26.800
the pharmaceutical company producing that, there&nbsp;
we see that many companies are moving in line with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:26.800 --> 02:05:34.640
us. I think we can be reassured that probably one&nbsp;
of the earliest parts of medicine that we will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:34.640 --> 02:05:41.920
able to tackle in net-zero will be anaesthesia.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:41.920 --> 02:05:45.360
Mike?
&gt;&gt;Mike Coupe: Nick, just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:45.360 --> 02:05:50.480
congratulate you on the presentation of the work&nbsp;
that you’ve done so far and work to come up in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:50.480 --> 02:05:56.080
future. In a previous life I made a very similar&nbsp;
commitment and I reckon at the time we made it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:56.080 --> 02:05:59.920
we probably knew about half of what needed to&nbsp;
be done, and the other half was an act of faith.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:59.920 --> 02:06:04.800
I’m just curious to know what are the big-ticket&nbsp;
items that you don’t think you know the answer to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:04.800 --> 02:06:09.040
what are the three or four things that you worry&nbsp;
about the technologies coming through and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:09.040 --> 02:06:13.120
kind of timescales you’re talking about&nbsp;
to actually crack those particular nuts?&nbsp;

02:06:14.480 --> 02:06:18.320
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: Good question, because I think&nbsp;
we need to be very, very clear, we don’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:18.320 --> 02:06:23.680
all of the answers today. What we have is a&nbsp;
long-term strategy, we have the mechanisms in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:23.680 --> 02:06:29.120
place to come back and revisit again and again.
And we have the first five years. We know very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:29.120 --> 02:06:33.200
very clearly what we need to be able to do in the&nbsp;
first five years and the first six months to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:33.200 --> 02:06:39.120
this programme going and move from talking about&nbsp;
talking to delivering kilotons of CO2 reduced.&nbsp;

02:06:41.920 --> 02:06:48.720
Probably one of the biggest areas I think that we&nbsp;
will run into, which I must say is not necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:48.720 --> 02:06:52.800
something the NHS will be able to solve by itself&nbsp;
is when you start to talk about some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:52.800 --> 02:06:56.800
hard materials, right? When you start to talk&nbsp;
about concrete, you start talking about steel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:56.800 --> 02:07:02.080
you start to talk about some of the industrial&nbsp;
processes required there. That’s where things&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:02.080 --> 02:07:06.400
get particularly challenging. And indeed, if you&nbsp;
go and look closely at the net-zero strategy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:06.400 --> 02:07:09.840
we leave a small proportion that we are&nbsp;
unsure how we’re going to deal with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:10.720 --> 02:07:18.960
About 50% of that is in the sort of construction&nbsp;
industry. I think we should be reassured that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:18.960 --> 02:07:22.960
there is good progress happening there. It’s&nbsp;
just got a big question mark over the top of it.&nbsp;

02:07:23.680 --> 02:07:30.080
There are other areas of uncertainty. There I&nbsp;
think we are looking towards a pretty rapidly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:30.080 --> 02:07:35.040
developing field of research and innovation in&nbsp;
this world. We have had some great success as I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:35.040 --> 02:07:39.440
already mentioned work on forthcoming&nbsp;
zero emission ambulances that I mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:39.440 --> 02:07:46.800
on anaesthetic harvesting and recycling. We&nbsp;
have as well working with within the NHS with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:46.800 --> 02:07:54.560
IRS and with the small business innovation&nbsp;
team, SBRI, a competition being launched for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:55.840 --> 02:08:01.840
1.1 million trying to spark innovation to answer&nbsp;
some of those questions. That is being launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:01.840 --> 02:08:08.640
today. The first successful competitors out the&nbsp;
other side of that we will have in place by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:08.640 --> 02:08:13.920
UN Climate Change Conference in November.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:13.920 --> 02:08:20.320
David, if I could just add to that briefly. So&nbsp;
in our report that we published six months ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:20.320 --> 02:08:26.000
we were able to show line of sight&nbsp;
to something like 85 to 90% of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:26.000 --> 02:08:30.720
actions that would be necessary to achieve the&nbsp;
goal, particularly in relation to direct care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:32.960 --> 02:08:39.200
The remaining 10 to 15%, the final mile&nbsp;
problem, we don’t have an obvious answer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:39.200 --> 02:08:44.400
yet. And that’s where the research will be&nbsp;
critical. And on the wider supply chain, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:44.400 --> 02:08:49.840
will clearly be much more challenging. So I think&nbsp;
we actually have grounds for optimism that we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:49.840 --> 02:08:55.760
actually an awful lot about what needs to be done.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And Tim, Tim Ferriss, I think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:55.760 --> 02:09:00.080
digital transformation programme is obviously&nbsp;
going to play a big part in this as well. If we&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:00.080 --> 02:09:05.440
can reduce people having to go into hospital so&nbsp;
much it or see the GP it makes a huge difference.&nbsp;

02:09:07.120 --> 02:09:10.000
Nick, thank you very much&nbsp;
indeed. You’re doing a great job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:10.000 --> 02:09:14.080
Keep at it, and you’ll come back&nbsp;
and see us every six months at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:14.080 --> 02:09:16.080
board, if that’s all right.
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:16.080 --> 02:09:22.240
thank you for having me. Thanks very much.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. So Ian, is Bola with us?&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:24.080 --> 02:09:28.400
I think you are with us.
&gt;&gt;Bola Owolabi: Hello. Hello. Hi.&nbsp;

02:09:28.400 --> 02:09:32.960
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Hello. I think you probably&nbsp;
know most people on the board but do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:32.960 --> 02:09:36.800
want to you want to kick off and just&nbsp;
introduce yourself first, please?&nbsp;

02:09:36.800 --> 02:09:42.000
&gt;&gt;Bola Owolabi: Thank you David and thank you&nbsp;
for having me. I’m Bola Owolabi, I’m health&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:42.000 --> 02:09:48.320
inequalities director and I’m also a general&nbsp;
practitioner in the Midlands. It’s good to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:48.320 --> 02:09:52.720
here. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:52.720 --> 02:09:56.480
So, Ian, are you going to kick off or is Bola&nbsp;
going to go straight into the presentation?&nbsp;

02:09:56.480 --> 02:10:03.520
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Just very briefly for me to say look,&nbsp;
I’m very pleased to take on the inequality brief&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:04.800 --> 02:10:12.640
last summer. And I particularly wanted to thank&nbsp;
Owen Williams, who has done so much to kickstart&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:12.640 --> 02:10:15.600
our work in this area, chairing an expert group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:16.320 --> 02:10:24.560
and coming out with a report of immediate steps&nbsp;
the NHS could take, rather than focus extensively&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:24.560 --> 02:10:29.600
on health inequalities, and the why the&nbsp;
actions we would like others to take,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:30.560 --> 02:10:37.280
Owen’s report is very practical about the things&nbsp;
that the NHS itself could do and do quickly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:38.720 --> 02:10:43.760
I think his role there and his ongoing&nbsp;
role in chairing our inequalities kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:44.640 --> 02:10:54.080
board has been incredibly important, as has&nbsp;
the arrival of Bola in January, in leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:54.080 --> 02:11:00.480
the inequalities team and bringing a huge amount&nbsp;
of energy and rigour to this particular space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:01.040 --> 02:11:06.400
And with which introduction, I will hand back to&nbsp;
Bola to run through the work that’s gone on, both&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:06.400 --> 02:11:11.920
what’s been achieved and what’s in the pipeline.
&gt;&gt;Bola Owolabi: Thank you. Thank you, Ian. So I’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:11.920 --> 02:11:19.360
start by saying that we all recognise that health&nbsp;
inequalities are not new. And the commitment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:19.360 --> 02:11:27.440
the NHS to tackling them predates the pandemic,&nbsp;
as set out within the NHS long term plan. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:27.440 --> 02:11:36.160
recognise the 19.6 years healthy life expectancy&nbsp;
gap between people in the most deprived areas of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:36.160 --> 02:11:43.440
the country and the less deprived areas. We also&nbsp;
recognise the 10-to-15-year life expectancy gap&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:43.440 --> 02:11:48.560
for people living with learning disability&nbsp;
and/or autism, and indeed people with severe&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:48.560 --> 02:11:55.920
mental illness compared to the national average.
And so within that context, the pandemic then&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:55.920 --> 02:12:02.240
further highlighted the imperative of accelerating&nbsp;
our action to tackle health inequalities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:02.800 --> 02:12:08.560
given the disproportionate impacts are on people&nbsp;
in the most deprived areas, and indeed, people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:08.560 --> 02:12:16.320
from ethnic minority communities. We noticed that&nbsp;
the likelihood of contracting COVID-19 infection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:16.320 --> 02:12:23.200
the likelihood of having severe disease, of&nbsp;
requiring Intensive Care Unit support, and indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:23.200 --> 02:12:29.680
the higher mortality rates that we’ve observed in&nbsp;
these particular communities, which has increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:29.680 --> 02:12:35.040
our commitment and focus on tackling health&nbsp;
inequalities, as Simon set out in his phase three&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:35.040 --> 02:12:40.720
pandemic response letter, setting out the eight&nbsp;
urgent actions for tackling health inequalities.&nbsp;

02:12:40.720 --> 02:12:45.280
And so when we consider that, it’s important&nbsp;
to then reflect on the roles of the NHS.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:46.080 --> 02:12:50.880
We also know that most of the drivers of health&nbsp;
inequalities are the social determinants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:51.600 --> 02:12:58.400
which by definition, the NHS cannot fix&nbsp;
independently. However, the NHS plays a powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:58.400 --> 02:13:03.680
role in narrowing health inequalities. First of&nbsp;
all, as an anchor institution in its own right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:04.320 --> 02:13:10.160
the abiding presence in most of our communities,&nbsp;
the biggest employer, the biggest owner of estate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:10.160 --> 02:13:14.960
and indeed the biggest buyer.
We also have the key role as a key&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:14.960 --> 02:13:21.040
partner within the integrated care system,&nbsp;
working with our multi-agency partners&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:21.040 --> 02:13:26.400
to really drive forward action on those wider&nbsp;
determinants that I was talking about earlier.&nbsp;

02:13:26.400 --> 02:13:33.200
However, our key and direct role is in tackling&nbsp;
inequalities in health care provision. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:33.200 --> 02:13:40.320
is the anchor point for our vision of exceptional&nbsp;
quality health care for all, functional phrase&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:40.320 --> 02:13:47.440
being for all, and that’s by ensuring equitable&nbsp;
access, excellent experience and optimal outcomes.&nbsp;

02:13:47.440 --> 02:13:52.640
And so against that backdrop, just to focus on the&nbsp;
progress that we’ve made in the last six months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:52.640 --> 02:13:58.880
we’ve galvanised action and built momentum&nbsp;
across the NHS and indeed beyond the NHS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:59.520 --> 02:14:04.720
by setting out a clear vision, and a shared&nbsp;
purpose for addressing health inequalities&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:04.720 --> 02:14:08.480
as a collective cause rather than a niche agenda.&nbsp;

02:14:08.480 --> 02:14:13.680
We’ve made significant progress in the&nbsp;
completeness of our health inequalities data&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:13.680 --> 02:14:17.680
through the development of our health&nbsp;
inequalities improvement dashboard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:17.680 --> 02:14:23.280
the minimum viable product element of that.&nbsp;
We’ve strengthened leadership at all levels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:23.920 --> 02:14:29.680
all regions, all integrated care systems and&nbsp;
providers have identified health inequalities&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:29.680 --> 02:14:36.080
senior responsible officers, and executive leads.
We’ve also established a specific health and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:36.080 --> 02:14:42.000
equalities improvement team under my leadership&nbsp;
and Ian’s executive sponsorship. We’ve also&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:42.000 --> 02:14:47.840
launched the NHS health anchors learning network&nbsp;
in collaboration with the Health Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:47.840 --> 02:14:53.840
and we’re taking forward a series of projects&nbsp;
across the country with the anchors framework.&nbsp;

02:14:54.480 --> 02:15:00.640
We’ve also set out within the operational planning&nbsp;
guidance, our five key priorities for continuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:00.640 --> 02:15:08.080
to accelerate the fast and fair recovery that&nbsp;
Amanda referenced earlier in her introductions.&nbsp;

02:15:08.080 --> 02:15:14.400
And I will say as Emily outlined earlier, our most&nbsp;
intense and successful work in narrowing health&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:14.400 --> 02:15:20.960
inequalities has to be the work in the COVID&nbsp;
vaccination programme, ensuring equitable access&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:20.960 --> 02:15:27.040
to the vaccines, as evidenced by the fact that&nbsp;
we’ve gone from 38% uptake in the Black African&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:27.040 --> 02:15:34.480
community back in January, to a position of 70%&nbsp;
by May this year. And also, as mentioned earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:34.480 --> 02:15:41.600
on the national achievement of 73.5% annual&nbsp;
health checks for people with learning disability&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:42.320 --> 02:15:46.960
two years ahead of target, is another&nbsp;
key achievement that I want to highlight.&nbsp;

02:15:47.600 --> 02:15:55.120
And as we go forward, just to outline the key next&nbsp;
steps. The first one is to redouble our efforts&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:55.120 --> 02:15:59.280
in making sure that our health&nbsp;
inequalities data are timely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:59.280 --> 02:16:05.520
they are accurate, they are complete, and most&nbsp;
importantly, that they give our systems actionable&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:05.520 --> 02:16:11.200
insight to be able to actually make tangible&nbsp;
improvements in narrowing health inequalities.&nbsp;

02:16:11.760 --> 02:16:15.120
Secondly, we will continue to&nbsp;
drive forward on those five key&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:15.120 --> 02:16:20.000
priorities that I’ve referenced earlier on.
And finally, we’re going to focus particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:20.640 --> 02:16:28.000
on that core 20%. So that is the 20%&nbsp;
most deprived communities, and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:28.000 --> 02:16:33.760
focusing on our ethnic minority communities,&nbsp;
particularly around five key priority areas.&nbsp;

02:16:33.760 --> 02:16:39.920
One is hypertension case finding, those 4.5&nbsp;
million cases that we haven’t yet found,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:39.920 --> 02:16:45.760
that are contributing to, you know, the growing&nbsp;
incidence of heart attacks that is showing up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:45.760 --> 02:16:52.399
our A&amp;Es. Also, early cancer diagnosis get into a&nbsp;
place where we’re finding most of our cancers at&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:52.399 --> 02:16:59.519
stage one and two, and getting to 75% for that.
Thirdly, is chronic respiratory disease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:59.520 --> 02:17:05.120
particularly COPD, and taking action in&nbsp;
reducing their readmission rates that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:05.120 --> 02:17:10.720
being driven largely by the exacerbations of&nbsp;
COPD, by ensuring that people are accessing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:10.720 --> 02:17:15.520
their flu and COVID vaccination.
As has been talked about earlier on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:15.520 --> 02:17:19.680
we’re also going to redouble efforts in&nbsp;
terms of our long-term plan commitment&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:19.680 --> 02:17:25.680
around the annual health checks for people with&nbsp;
severe mental illness. And finally, on maternity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:25.680 --> 02:17:31.280
it’s to accelerate action to ensure that&nbsp;
continuity of maternity care, as again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:31.280 --> 02:17:35.920
we have committed to within the long-term&nbsp;
plan, because we have the evidence to tell us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:35.920 --> 02:17:41.200
it makes a significant difference in terms of&nbsp;
maternal mortality, and indeed, preterm births.&nbsp;

02:17:41.760 --> 02:17:46.319
So just a quick introduction in terms&nbsp;
of the progress so far, and what we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:46.319 --> 02:17:51.200
intending to do in the coming period.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Bola, thank you very&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:51.200 --> 02:17:56.559
much. That was a great presentation. As always,&nbsp;
when you give a presentation, it was absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:56.560 --> 02:18:01.760
on the button there. So, thank you. Wol?
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Thanks, David, and thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:01.760 --> 02:18:05.600
Bola for the presentation. And thank you&nbsp;
for the hard work you’re putting into&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:06.640 --> 02:18:12.479
making progress in this area. I’m going to&nbsp;
be slightly more challenging than perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:12.479 --> 02:18:17.040
we’ve set out there because there’s a bit in your&nbsp;
paper which is absolutely correct but actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:17.040 --> 02:18:21.840
I think sort of slightly ducks the issue. And I&nbsp;
do recognise we’re not trying to boil the ocean.&nbsp;

02:18:21.840 --> 02:18:25.120
So what I mean, you rightly say that actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:26.080 --> 02:18:29.840
the fundamental factors driving health&nbsp;
inequalities are not the responsibility of NHS.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:30.720 --> 02:18:35.280
And you’re rightly focusing initially on&nbsp;
provision. But actually, I think it misses&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:35.280 --> 02:18:39.840
a trick and this is a trick because basically,&nbsp;
the NHS is the biggest actor in most places.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:41.040 --> 02:18:47.439
And we’ve been very, very reticent to act on our&nbsp;
own to actually address some issues. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:47.439 --> 02:18:50.479
you’re acting through I think the Health&nbsp;
Promotion Agency whenever it turns up.&nbsp;

02:18:51.040 --> 02:18:57.519
To give you a very concrete example, I’ll borrow&nbsp;
from the organisation I chair, being Guys and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:57.520 --> 02:19:04.560
Thomas’s Foundation, which Amanda knows very well.&nbsp;
In Lambeth and Southwark, an urban deprived area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:04.560 --> 02:19:08.000
lots of health inequalities, one&nbsp;
of the worst places in the country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:08.720 --> 02:19:15.920
And acting with data, insight to address multiple&nbsp;
long-term conditions for example. So one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:15.920 --> 02:19:21.600
most egregious things happening is the progression&nbsp;
from one conditioning highlight to many.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:23.120 --> 02:19:26.559
And we still don’t understand enough about how&nbsp;
that happens. And we’ve been at it for four years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:27.280 --> 02:19:31.759
Okay, the NHS is the biggest actor in this area,&nbsp;
it could do more to make that big difference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:31.760 --> 02:19:36.880
because it accounts for an enormous amount&nbsp;
of what actually in terms of cost when people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:36.880 --> 02:19:43.520
present with three or four multiple conditions.
The second point is around using other people so&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:43.520 --> 02:19:50.560
we’ve got Mike here from JS or J&nbsp;
Sainsbury’s was his last organisation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:50.560 --> 02:19:57.520
Actually using them to basically understand things&nbsp;
like obesity, which again present in places like&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:57.520 --> 02:20:02.160
Lambeth and Southwark very, very heavily,&nbsp;
actually saying, your ranging which we did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:02.160 --> 02:20:08.800
is appalling in Lambeth and Southwark, relative&nbsp;
to other places, and actually sitting, we did&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:08.800 --> 02:20:14.960
the work, we did the work and actually it did&nbsp;
happen. Like, where we basically, what you were&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:14.960 --> 02:20:19.760
presenting to people meant the environment they&nbsp;
were operating in was that much more difficult to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:19.760 --> 02:20:24.240
actually make healthy options, healthy choices,&nbsp;
this is going back four or five years ago.&nbsp;

02:20:25.040 --> 02:20:30.080
Again, with the heft of the NHS behind this, it&nbsp;
could make a big difference. Actually, there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:30.080 --> 02:20:34.800
a small charity trying to make a difference there.
And my final point is, we’ve got a coming crisis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:34.800 --> 02:20:41.120
which we know, long COVID. We know long&nbsp;
COVID basically, the incidence of COVID on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:41.120 --> 02:20:45.439
the communities we’re talking about&nbsp;
are huge. Are we looking forward enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:46.080 --> 02:20:52.559
to say what we are going to do about that?
And again, long COVID, dreadful term, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:52.560 --> 02:20:58.319
doesn’t present as I understand it in an easy&nbsp;
way. It’ll be multiple visits through multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:58.319 --> 02:21:03.759
different things before someone actually goes, you&nbsp;
know what, maybe you’ve got long Covid. So again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:03.760 --> 02:21:10.000
I can see very good work today. But the challenges&nbsp;
are sort of multiplying and continuing. So I urge&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:10.000 --> 02:21:14.319
you to redouble your efforts and ask Simon to&nbsp;
sign off before he leaves on tripling your budget.&nbsp;

02:21:16.319 --> 02:21:22.080
&gt;&gt;Bola Owolabi: Thank you, Wol, for that&nbsp;
comment, and I’m pleased that Simon is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:22.080 --> 02:21:28.960
smiling. I’m going to take that as acquiescence.
But I will just focus on your three key points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:28.960 --> 02:21:34.480
if I may. So your point about multimorbidity&nbsp;
is very well made. And I’m pleased to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:35.200 --> 02:21:40.080
prior to taking on this director role, I was&nbsp;
national specialty advisor for older people and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:40.080 --> 02:21:45.519
integrated care to NHS England and Improvement&nbsp;
and lead on the anticipatory care framework,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:46.080 --> 02:21:51.920
which will be going live later on this year,&nbsp;
which absolutely focuses on that person centred,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:51.920 --> 02:21:57.200
holistic care of people, particularly&nbsp;
those living with multimorbidity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:57.840 --> 02:22:02.319
with additional investment into&nbsp;
primary care, to especially focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:02.319 --> 02:22:05.120
on multimorbidity through the&nbsp;
anticipatory care approach.&nbsp;

02:22:05.760 --> 02:22:10.560
So just to give some assurance that we are&nbsp;
absolutely focused on that as an agenda.&nbsp;

02:22:11.280 --> 02:22:17.040
Secondly, in terms of the prevention&nbsp;
agenda, particularly around obesity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:17.040 --> 02:22:23.840
again, by way of assurance, to say that we have&nbsp;
an entire prevention programme within NHSE&amp;I that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:23.840 --> 02:22:30.080
has a very clear obesity strategy, which again,&nbsp;
has to be delivered in collaboration, as you say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:30.080 --> 02:22:36.160
with other system partners, not just the NHS&nbsp;
functioning as a siloed actor in that space.&nbsp;

02:22:36.160 --> 02:22:43.680
And finally, around long COVID, I’m really proud&nbsp;
to say we published the NHS long COVID plan just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:43.680 --> 02:22:48.399
over a week ago with an entire section&nbsp;
committed to how we’re going to ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:48.399 --> 02:22:53.679
equitable access to our long COVID service&nbsp;
with a complete health inequalities matrix&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:54.560 --> 02:22:58.720
right at the heart and the centre of the&nbsp;
long COVID plan. And I also chair the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:58.720 --> 02:23:04.800
long COVID health inequalities workstream as a&nbsp;
commitment from the NHS to making sure that yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:04.800 --> 02:23:10.000
we provide the service, but we do so in a&nbsp;
way that gives people equitable access, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:10.000 --> 02:23:14.960
that once they cross the threshold, they have an&nbsp;
excellent experience of the care that we provide.&nbsp;

02:23:14.960 --> 02:23:19.280
I don’t know if Ian wants to build on any of&nbsp;
that further, but just to give you assurances&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:19.280 --> 02:23:22.080
across those three areas.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Bola,&nbsp;&nbsp;

